-- postpartum week 61: Eating well while you're grieving (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   61,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well while you''re grieving',
   '"Grief can take your appetite; it doesn''t have to take your care."',
   'Grief reorganizes a day the same way illness or upheaval does — plans lose their shape, ordinary tasks feel heavier, and eating is often one of the first things to become difficult, whether that means barely wanting food or reaching for it constantly. Grief, stress, disrupted sleep and changes in daily routine can all affect appetite. You may barely want food, eat more often, or turn toward familiar comfort foods — and none of these responses means you are grieving "wrong." If eating very little continues for more than a few days, or comes with noticeable weight loss, it''s worth mentioning to a doctor — not because grief itself is a medical problem, but because your body still needs fuel to get through it. This week doesn''t ask you to eat perfectly, or even normally. It offers small, low-effort ways to keep feeding yourself through a stretch of time when very little feels manageable.',
   'If you manage a condition such as diabetes, thyroid disease or high blood pressure: grief can disrupt appetite, sleep and routine in ways that affect these conditions. Keep your usual monitoring and medication schedule as steady as you can, and tell your care team if intake has changed substantially. If you''re pregnant or breastfeeding while grieving: your usual pregnancy or breastfeeding nutrition and hydration needs continue even on days when eating feels impossible — small, frequent bites and fluids matter more than a complete meal. If you monitor blood glucose: choose unsweetened porridge or dalia, leave out jaggery, and pair fruit or toast with curd, milk, egg, paneer, nuts or another protein you tolerate. Continue following your individual glucose and medication plan even if your portions are smaller than usual — the A and B meal options in this document aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which specific dishes fit your own targets. Please seek urgent help now if you are thinking about harming yourself, feel unable to keep yourself safe, or cannot manage immediate basic care. Contact your local emergency service or urgent mental-health service, and tell someone you trust who can stay with you. You do not have to manage that moment alone. You do not need to wait for a particular timeline before asking for support: if grief is making everyday life, sleep, eating or caring for yourself increasingly difficult, speak with a doctor, therapist or grief counsellor. Persistent and disabling grief can sometimes need more structured support, but only a qualified professional can assess that. There is no timeline you''re supposed to be on, and no "right" way to eat through this. Small, imperfect steps are still steps.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; you don''t need six different dishes to get through the week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli or cheela is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Grief reorganizes a day fast, the same way illness or a crisis does. Ordinary routines — including meals — often become the first casualty. This week doesn''t ask you to eat perfectly; it offers small, manageable ways to keep feeding yourself.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela (2 pieces) with coriander chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted almonds (15g) with a banana and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of warm milk (200ml) with a pinch of nutmeg", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A banana"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "When food has no appeal at all", "notes": "A vanished appetite is one of the most common responses to grief. It isn''t a sign that something is wrong with you, and it isn''t something to push through with willpower alone. Worth knowing: Start smaller than feels necessary — a few bites, a smoothie, a slice of toast. Building up from small, frequent amounts is often easier than aiming for a full meal. Pre-made, frozen or ready-to-eat food removes a step you may not have the energy for right now, and that''s a reasonable choice, not a shortcut to feel guilty about. If eating very little continues for more than a few days, or comes with noticeable weight loss, mention it to a doctor.", "breakfast_a": "A smoothie with milk (200ml), banana and a spoon of peanut butter (15g), if that''s easier than a plate", "breakfast_b": "Buttered toast (2 slices) with a small bowl of plain curd (150g) — start with whichever part feels manageable", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with a spoon of ghee, in whatever amount feels manageable", "lunch_b": "Dal soup (1 cup) with soft rice (3/4 cup cooked) and a spoon of curd", "nourishment_break_a": "A few spoons of curd (100g) with a banana", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Soft rice (3/4 cup cooked) with a thin dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "A boiled egg with toast (2 slices), if you can manage it", "still_hungry": "Whatever small amount feels possible right now — it counts"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "When food becomes the thing that holds you together", "notes": "For some people, grief pulls the other way — toward food as comfort, routine or distraction. That''s just as normal a response as losing your appetite, and this week isn''t about correcting it. Worth knowing: Eating for comfort during a genuinely hard stretch isn''t a failure of discipline. If you notice it becoming the only way you''re managing difficult feelings, that''s worth mentioning to a doctor or counsellor — not as a verdict on you, but as one more form of support available.", "breakfast_a": "Wheat dalia (1 cup) with jaggery and milk (200ml)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-potato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of rice kheer (150g, made with milk) with a few crushed almonds", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with roasted chana (30g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-potato curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Letting people feed you", "notes": "If someone offers to cook, bring a meal, or take on a task so you can rest, accepting that help is not a failure to cope — it''s one of the most practical things grief support looks like. A meal someone else made, reheated and eaten straight from the container, is still a real meal.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted walnuts (15g) with a guava and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml) with roasted jeera", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A banana"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If grief doesn''t loosen its grip", "notes": "Grief naturally eases in intensity over time for most people, even while it never fully disappears. For some, it stays acute and disabling for far longer, in ways that don''t resolve on their own. Worth knowing: You do not need to wait for a particular timeline before asking for support. If grief is making everyday life, sleep, eating or caring for yourself increasingly difficult, speak with a doctor, therapist or grief counsellor. Persistent and disabling grief can sometimes need more structured support, but only a qualified professional can assess that.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge (1 cup, with milk, 200ml) and a banana", "breakfast_b": "Boiled eggs (2) with toast (2 slices), an orange and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong-masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-tomato curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with finely ground sunflower seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Prawn curry (100g prawns) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Grief alongside everything else you''re carrying", "notes": "Grief doesn''t pause the rest of your life. You may be doing the quiet, exhausting work of holding several things at once — your own loss and everything else that still needs you. That deserves real food and real support, not just extra willpower.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with a tomato-onion salad and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-mushroom toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-spinach curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a spoon of soft date paste", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Mutton-and-capsicum curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Is there one small, low-effort way of feeding yourself this week that felt more possible than the others? That''s worth keeping, for now.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela (2 pieces) with coriander chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted almonds (15g) with a banana and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of warm milk (200ml) with a pinch of nutmeg", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A banana"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to let feeding yourself through this be enough, without needing it to look like eating "well" in the usual sense?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: caring for yourself doesn''t pause for hard seasons; it just may need to look smaller and gentler than usual.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 62: Eating well as you start to feel like yourself again (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   62,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well as you start to feel like yourself again',
   '"Feeling like yourself again isn''t about going back — it''s about enjoying who you''re becoming now."',
   'Somewhere in the second year, many mothers notice something shifting — a bit more energy, a flicker of interest in things beyond the next feed or nap, food that tastes like more than fuel again. It''s a genuinely good sign, and it''s worth noticing without suspicion. The transition into motherhood is sometimes called matrescence. It can involve lasting changes in identity, relationships, priorities and the way you experience your own needs. There is no single timeline for that transition, and a medical recovery milestone does not mark the end of it. Food is one of the ordinary places this shows up: wanting to cook something for the pleasure of it again, missing a favourite dish, looking forward to a meal out. None of that needs to be earned or justified — enjoying food again is allowed to just be enjoyable. If it isn''t happening yet, that''s common too. This timeline varies enormously, and there''s no schedule you''re behind on.',
   'If persistent low mood, or loss of interest or pleasure in things you used to enjoy — including food — continues for weeks and doesn''t lift: this is worth raising with a doctor. Depression or anxiety can persist into — or arise during — the second year after birth. Whatever label a clinician uses, persistent low mood, anxiety or loss of interest deserves support. If you''re pregnant again or breastfeeding while this shift is still underway: continue your usual nutrition needs for that — this identity shift and a physical recovery timeline don''t have to move at the same pace. If you manage a chronic condition such as diabetes, thyroid disease or high blood pressure: as energy and appetite shift, your usual monitoring and treatment plan still applies — mention any noticeable change in intake or activity to your care team. If you monitor blood glucose: choose unsweetened milk, curd, porridge and drinks; pair fruit or toast with protein; and replace sabudana khichdi with vegetable poha or besan chilla if that works better with your individual glucose plan. The A and B meal options in this document aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which specific dishes fit your own targets. If you manage high blood pressure: choose unsalted nuts and seeds, and use roasted cumin, mint or lemon instead of chaat masala when you need to limit sodium. Please seek urgent help now if you are thinking about harming yourself, feel unable to keep yourself safe, or cannot manage immediate basic care. Contact your local emergency service or urgent mental-health service, and tell someone you trust who can stay with you. There''s no single moment when you''ll feel "back to normal," because normal isn''t where this is headed — it''s somewhere new, and you''re allowed to enjoy getting there.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; you don''t need six different dishes to get through the week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli or dosa is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Somewhere in this stretch, many mothers notice a bit more energy or curiosity returning — including around food. If that''s starting to happen for you, this week is about welcoming it, not questioning it.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted peanuts (15g) with a guava and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml) with roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A banana"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Rediscovering foods you used to love", "notes": "A craving for a specific dish, a memory of a favourite restaurant, a sudden urge to cook something just because you want to — these are ordinary signs of appetite for life returning, not indulgences to feel wary of.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a capsicum sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a capsicum sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with finely ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with roti (2 pieces) and a cauliflower sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Cooking as something other than a chore", "notes": "If you have a bit more energy or time than you did earlier, cooking can become something other than one more task to get through — a new recipe, an ingredient you haven''t used in a while, something made purely because it sounded good. If cooking still feels like one more thing to manage right now, that''s fine too — this is an invitation, not a new obligation.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-capsicum curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Eating out and feeling normal again", "notes": "Eating out can feel very different from the anxious, exhausting version of it in the earlier months. If it''s starting to feel more like an outing you enjoy than a logistics exercise to survive, that''s worth noticing. Worth knowing: For your own food safety when eating out: choose freshly cooked food served hot, and avoid unfamiliar ice, raw chutneys or uncooked accompaniments where food or water safety is uncertain. One enjoyable restaurant meal does not need to be nutritionally perfect.", "breakfast_a": "Masala dosa (1 large) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cheese toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor-masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted chana (30g) with a mandarin and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml) with a pinch of chaat masala", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "When \"feeling like yourself\" still feels far off", "notes": "The transition into motherhood does not follow one standard timetable. If \"feeling like yourself\" still feels far off, that does not mean you are behind. Worth knowing: If low mood, or a loss of interest in things you''d normally enjoy, has continued for weeks and shows no sign of lifting, it''s worth speaking with a doctor. Depression or anxiety can persist into — or arise during — the second year after birth. Whatever label a clinician uses, persistent low mood, anxiety or loss of interest deserves support. Please seek urgent help now if you are thinking about harming yourself, feel unable to keep yourself safe, or cannot manage immediate basic care — contact your local emergency service or urgent mental-health service, and tell someone you trust who can stay with you.", "breakfast_a": "Oats idli (3 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-carrot toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with chia seeds soaked until fully softened", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Making space for you at the table", "notes": "Making space for yourself at a table — with a partner, a friend, or just alone with a meal you chose — is not a small or frivolous thing. It''s a real, ordinary way of reclaiming a bit of your own life.", "breakfast_a": "Sabudana khichdi (1 cup) with finely ground peanuts mixed through and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a spoon of smooth cashew paste stirred in", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a lobia dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Is there one small food-related pleasure — a dish, a restaurant, a way of cooking — that used to be yours, and that you might make room for again this week?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted peanuts (15g) with a guava and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml) with roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A banana"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to let "feeling like yourself again" be about becoming, not returning — and to let food be part of that pleasure rather than a test of it?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: this stretch of motherhood keeps changing, and your relationship with food gets to change right along with it, toward more ease, not less.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
