-- postpartum week 63: Eating well when you're expecting again (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   63,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when you''re expecting again',
   '"This pregnancy gets to be its own — even while you''re still caring for the one before it."',
   'This week''s pregnancy-specific guidance applies only if you are pregnant again. If you''re not, continue with the regular maternal meal options elsewhere in this series — none of the prenatal-specific instructions below need to apply to you. If you are pregnant, use guidance appropriate to your current pregnancy stage; a first-trimester need is different from a third-trimester one. Finding out you''re expecting again is a different moment from thinking about whether to — this week is for the mothers who now are, while still very much in the middle of caring for the child they already have. Some nutrient needs rise again with this new pregnancy: folate intake matters from very early on, and iron needs increase, particularly worth attention if your levels hadn''t fully recovered from your last pregnancy or birth. If you conceived on the shorter side of the interval your care team may have discussed with you, that''s simply worth mentioning at your next appointment — the research on how much a short gap between pregnancies affects nutrient stores is still evolving, not a settled verdict on what will happen to you. Your needs depend partly on how far along this pregnancy is. In a singleton pregnancy, people who began pregnancy at a healthy weight generally do not need routine extra calories in the first trimester; average needs rise by about 340 calories a day in the second trimester and about 450 in the third. These are general estimates, not a target every mother must hit. Your starting weight, activity, medical conditions, nausea and whether you are still breastfeeding can all change what is appropriate for you. If you are not already taking an appropriate prenatal supplement, contact your maternity clinician or pharmacist promptly. Standard guidance commonly includes a prenatal vitamin containing at least 400 micrograms of folic acid, particularly before conception and during the first 12 weeks. Some medical histories require a different prescribed dose. Do not take two prenatal or postnatal multivitamins together, because excess amounts of some nutrients can be harmful.',
   'Contact your maternity clinician promptly for vaginal bleeding or new abdominal or pelvic pain. Seek emergency care for sudden or severe abdominal or pelvic pain, shoulder pain, fainting, marked weakness or severe dizziness. If you''re still breastfeeding your older child: continuing through a healthy, uncomplicated pregnancy is generally reassuring, though the evidence is more limited for higher-risk pregnancies. Ask your maternity clinician for individual advice if you have bleeding, abdominal or uterine pain, contractions, a history or signs of preterm labour, recurrent pregnancy loss, a multiple pregnancy, poor weight gain or another pregnancy complication — these don''t automatically mean you must stop; your clinician can help you weigh your particular risks and your preference. If you conceived less than about 18 months after your last birth, or had a caesarean birth: mention the interval and your birth history at your next appointment — this is worth an individual conversation with your care team, not a fixed rule. If your iron levels hadn''t fully recovered from your last pregnancy or birth: ask about rechecking them now rather than assuming this pregnancy will be identical to your last — needs are rising again regardless of where you started. If you manage diabetes: pregnancy usually brings different glucose targets and medication needs. Pair rice, poha, toast or crackers with protein where tolerated, choose unsweetened drinks, and contact your diabetes or maternity team early rather than using your postpartum targets unchanged — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content either, so check which specific dishes fit your own targets. If you take levothyroxine: tell your prescriber promptly that you are pregnant, because monitoring and dosage may need review. Ask your pharmacist how to separate it from prenatal vitamins, iron or calcium; these can reduce levothyroxine absorption when taken together. If you manage high blood pressure or another chronic condition: a new pregnancy generally means updated monitoring and targets — check in with your care team rather than continuing your postpartum plan unchanged. There''s no need to have this pregnancy figured out yet. Steady meals, your prenatal supplement, and a little patience with yourself can support you through this stage of pregnancy.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs remain valuable protein and iron sources through this pregnancy, cooked fully. FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings of fish a week from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking — while avoiding higher-mercury species like king mackerel, shark, swordfish or tilefish. Follow local advisories.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; you don''t need six different dishes to get through the week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including during pregnancy — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Folate, iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention this pregnancy, especially if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "However this pregnancy arrived — planned for a while, or a surprise — it''s allowed to bring a mix of feelings alongside it. You do not need to overhaul your meals today, but arrange your early maternity review and follow pregnancy food and supplement precautions now.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-coriander toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a bottle gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bottle gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of roasted makhana (15g) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Restarting prenatal nutrition", "notes": "Folate and iron needs rise again with this pregnancy, much as they did with your last one. If you''re not already taking an appropriate prenatal supplement, contact your maternity clinician or pharmacist promptly. Standard guidance commonly includes a prenatal vitamin containing at least 400 micrograms of folic acid, especially before conception and through the first 12 weeks. If you are further along, continue your prenatal supplement as advised rather than assuming it is too late to benefit. Avoid taking a prenatal and a postnatal supplement together, since excess amounts of some nutrients can be harmful. Worth knowing: Iron-rich foods (dal, spinach, eggs, meat) alongside a source of vitamin C at the same meal help your body absorb iron better — worth doing whether or not your last iron levels had fully recovered.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices), orange segments and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a beetroot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beetroot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of finely ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a palak-chana curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Energy for two kinds of care", "notes": "If you are in the first trimester, you may not need a larger overall intake yet. If you are further along — or are also breastfeeding — your energy needs may be higher. Appetite and individual guidance matter more than forcing one standard portion. If your energy is low, letting a partner, family member, or friend take over a meal or a chunk of household work for a stretch isn''t a failure to manage things yourself — it''s a reasonable way to get through a demanding few weeks.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-methi toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-methi curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you''re still breastfeeding", "notes": "Available evidence is generally reassuring about continuing breastfeeding during a healthy, uncomplicated pregnancy, but the evidence is more limited for higher-risk pregnancies. Breastfeeding also adds to the nutritional and practical demands already placed on you. Worth knowing: Ask your maternity clinician for individual advice if you have vaginal bleeding, abdominal or uterine pain, contractions, a history or signs of preterm labour, recurrent pregnancy loss, a multiple pregnancy, poor weight gain or another pregnancy complication. These circumstances do not automatically mean you must stop; your clinician can help weigh your particular risks and your preference. Meeting both this pregnancy''s needs and ongoing breastfeeding at the same time asks more of you nutritionally — steady meals, fluids, and rest matter more than any single food choice.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-beetroot toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-spinach curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "When nausea makes eating hard", "notes": "Nausea, food aversions, and tiredness can return with this pregnancy much as they did before — and they''re not easier to manage the second time around simply because you already know them, especially alongside everything else that still needs your attention. Small, frequent, plain meals are often easier than three big ones right now — there''s no need to \"eat properly\" if a cracker and a banana is what actually stays down. Worth knowing: Contact your care team urgently if you cannot keep liquids down for more than about eight hours, cannot eat for 24 hours, are passing very little or very dark urine, feel persistently dizzy or faint, are losing weight, vomit blood, or have fever or significant abdominal pain. Seek emergency care if you faint, become confused or feel severely unwell.", "breakfast_a": "Plain toast (2 slices) with banana and a few spoons of curd (100g), if tolerated", "breakfast_b": "Curd rice (1 cup) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Plain rice (3/4 cup cooked) with a mild moong dal (3/4 cup)", "nourishment_break_a": "Plain crackers with curd (150g) or a small piece of paneer", "nourishment_break_b": "Lemon water without added sugar, with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a mild potato-and-pea sabzi", "dinner_b": "Plain rice (3/4 cup cooked) with a protein-forward mild dal (3/4 cup) and a steamed vegetable", "still_hungry": "Plain crackers with curd, milk or a small piece of paneer — whichever stays down"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Making room for you amid two sets of needs", "notes": "Between everything already on your plate and a new pregnancy''s needs, it''s easy for your own meals to become an afterthought again. Making room for a proper plate for yourself — not just whatever''s quickest to grab — is a genuinely worthwhile use of a few minutes today.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-pumpkin curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-methi curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a spoon of smooth almond butter stirred in", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-spinach dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. What''s one thing — help from someone, a slower morning, a meal you don''t have to cook yourself — that would make this stretch a little easier to carry?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-coriander toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a bottle gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bottle gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of roasted makhana (15g) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to let this pregnancy be its own experience — not a repeat of your last one, and not something to manage perfectly alongside everything else you''re already carrying?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: your own nourishment keeps mattering, whatever else is unfolding around it.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 64: Eating well when you're parenting solo for a stretch (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   64,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when you''re parenting solo for a stretch',
   '"You don''t have to cook well this week — you just have to get through it, fed."',
   'A work trip, an unusually demanding stretch at the office, a visiting relative who needed more care than you did — whatever is keeping your usual support away right now, parenting solo for a while genuinely changes what''s realistic to cook, eat, and manage. This week isn''t about finding a clever system to keep everything running exactly as before. It''s about getting through a demanding stretch reasonably well-fed, using shortcuts that would ordinarily feel like cutting corners but are simply sensible right now. Batch-cooking one dish in a bigger quantity and leaning on convenience staples without guilt are both genuinely sound strategies here, not compromises to feel apologetic about. However long this stretch lasts, your usual way of cooking and eating can simply pick back up once it passes.',
   'If you manage a chronic condition such as thyroid disease: keep taking your medication and monitoring on schedule even on the most demanding days this week — a solo stretch is exactly when it''s easiest, and least advisable, to let this slip. If you''re still breastfeeding: your own fluids and meals matter more, not less, right now — regular meals and fluids can help support your energy and nutritional needs. If you notice yourself skipping meals most days: that''s worth deliberately changing rather than accepting as how this stretch has to go — even a very simple meal eaten on a regular schedule is better than none. If you manage diabetes: avoid skipping meals and try to pair rice, roti, toast, or fruit with a protein or fibre-rich food. Continue following your prescribed monitoring and medication plan; do not rely on this general menu to replace it — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which specific dishes fit your own targets. If you manage high blood pressure: packaged foods, ready-made meals, pickles, papad, sauces, and takeaway dishes can be high in sodium. When practical, compare labels, choose lower-sodium options, request less salt, and balance convenience foods with fruit, curd, dal, or a simply cooked vegetable. If low mood, anxiety, or loss of interest persists, interferes with daily life, or feels difficult to manage: speak with a healthcare professional rather than waiting for this stretch to end. Seek urgent help for thoughts of harming yourself, feeling detached from reality, severe confusion, or hearing or seeing things others do not. There''s no version of this week that has to look impressive. Fed, on schedule, and through it is entirely enough.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs remain quick, low-effort protein sources this week, cooked fully. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; you don''t need six different dishes to get through the week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli or dosa is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Whether it''s a work trip, a demanding stretch at the office, or something else keeping your usual support away for now, parenting solo for a while is genuinely harder — not something you''re managing badly if it feels that way. This week lowers the bar on purpose.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-potato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml) with a pinch of black pepper", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a beetroot sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a beetroot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "One meal, made once, eaten twice", "notes": "Cooking one bigger batch of something and repeating it across a couple of meals is not a lesser way to feed yourself this week. It''s a genuinely sound way to get through it. A single one-pot dal, khichdi, or curry made in a larger quantity can cover today''s lunch and dinner with almost no extra effort the second time.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with coriander chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd — make enough for dinner too", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi — make enough curry for dinner too", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml) with a pinch of ajwain", "dinner_a": "The extra khichdi from lunch, reheated until steaming hot, with curd", "dinner_b": "The extra mutton curry from lunch, reheated until steaming hot, with roti (2 pieces) and any remaining beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "A real meal for you, served on purpose", "notes": "It''s easy for your own meals to shrink to whatever''s fastest to grab between everything else. Today''s about giving yourself a real plate on purpose — a freshly served portion or safely stored leftovers reheated properly are both a genuine meal — rather than picking at whatever''s left on someone else''s plate. Worth knowing: Keep a couple of ready-to-eat proteins on hand — boiled eggs, roasted chana, a block of paneer — so a real meal for you takes minutes, not another round of cooking.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dosa (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A bowl of curd (150g) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Keeping your own meals ready when there''s no spare hand", "notes": "Solo stretches are easier to get through when your own meals don''t depend on a free hand or a spare moment. A few ready components — cooked dal, boiled eggs, cut vegetables, a block of paneer — mean assembling a real meal for yourself takes minutes between everything else, not another round of cooking.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-mushroom toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beetroot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-capsicum curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "An apple with roasted walnuts (15g) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-beans curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-capsicum curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "When this week feels like genuinely too much", "notes": "Some days in a stretch like this will simply be harder than others, and that''s ordinary, not a sign you''re failing at it. A takeaway dinner, a frozen meal, or toast for the third time this week is a reasonable way through a genuinely demanding day. Worth knowing: Ordinary tiredness and overwhelm are expected here. If low mood, anxiety, or loss of interest persists, interferes with daily life, or feels difficult to manage, speak with a healthcare professional rather than waiting for this stretch to end. Seek urgent help for thoughts of harming yourself, feeling detached from reality, severe confusion, or hearing or seeing things others do not.", "breakfast_a": "Plain toast (2 slices) with peanut butter and a glass of milk (200ml)", "breakfast_b": "Curd rice (1 cup) with a boiled egg", "lunch_a": "Frozen or pre-made dal (3/4 cup) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a quick sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "Curd rice (1 cup) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml)", "dinner_a": "Takeaway or delivered food, whatever is easiest tonight — a protein alongside something plant-based is a nice bonus when easily available, but eating what''s manageable tonight is still enough", "dinner_b": "Roti (2 pieces) with a quick moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Letting someone else feed you for a change", "notes": "Accepting a cooked dish from a friend, a grocery delivery, or a relative''s offer to help isn''t a failure to manage on your own — it''s a genuinely sensible way to get through a stretch like this one. If someone offers to help, a specific answer — \"a cooked dinner on Thursday would help enormously\" — is often easier for them to act on than a general \"I''m fine, thank you.\"", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a spoon of smooth cashew paste stirred in", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. What''s one shortcut from this week — a batch-cooked dish, a convenience staple, a friend''s offer of help — that''s worth keeping even after this stretch ends?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-potato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml) with a pinch of black pepper", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a beetroot sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a beetroot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to measure this week by "we got through it, fed" rather than by how closely it resembled a normal week?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: good-enough, sustainable meals matter more than perfect ones, whatever this season of motherhood brings.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 65: Eating well when you and your partner don't agree on how the household eats (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   65,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when you and your partner don''t agree on how the household eats',
   '"You don''t need to agree on every dish — you need a household rhythm that keeps your own meals from disappearing in the disagreement."',
   'Every household blends two different upbringings around food — how much salt or spice feels normal, whose family''s habits quietly become "how we do things" in this house. It''s common for a mother to end up absorbing most of that compromise — cooking to everyone else''s preferences while her own appetite or favourite dishes quietly go unmet, meal after meal. None of this is really about whose family cooked "correctly." It''s about a shared household rhythm that doesn''t need everyone''s full agreement, and still leaves genuine room for your own plate. This week isn''t about resolving every difference with your partner or extended family. One practical step that helps regardless of who''s "right": a shared base — rice or roti everyone eats — with each person''s preferred protein side served separately, and salt or spice adjusted individually at the table rather than negotiated into one compromise dish.',
   'If you manage diabetes, thyroid disease, high blood pressure or another condition and household meals don''t always match your needs: your own monitoring, medication schedule and dietary requirements take priority over anyone else''s food preferences — say so plainly rather than quietly adjusting your plan around a shared menu. The A and B meal options in this document aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so if diabetes is part of your plan, check which specific dishes fit your own targets. If you''re still breastfeeding: keeping to a regular eating and fluid schedule for yourself matters even during a stretch where household meals feel like an ongoing negotiation. If persistent tension over food or household roles is affecting your mood, sleep, or sense of safety at home: that''s worth raising with a doctor, counsellor, or a trusted person, rather than something to manage alone indefinitely. A calmer table doesn''t require everyone to agree on everything — just enough shared ground that your own meals don''t quietly disappear in the disagreement.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety, whichever household''s tradition you''re drawing from. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; you don''t need six different dishes to get through the week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Every household blends two different upbringings around food — how much spice, how strict about home-cooked meals, whose family''s habits carry more weight in the kitchen. A running disagreement about whose way is right can quietly end up costing you your own meals, not just an argument at the table. Today''s just about noticing that pattern, not fixing it.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "When cooking to please everyone leaves your own plate last", "notes": "It''s easy to become the person who cooks everyone else''s preferences into every meal while your own favourite dish keeps getting postponed. Your own appetite and preferences are allowed a seat at that table too — not just whatever keeps the peace. Worth knowing: Cooking one dish that''s genuinely yours once or twice a week, even a simple one, is a reasonable way to keep your own eating from disappearing into everyone else''s preferences.", "breakfast_a": "Oats chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cheese bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lauki sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of finely ground sesame seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "When a food disagreement is standing in for something else", "notes": "A disagreement about how spicy the dal should be, or whether skipping a meal now and then is fine, is sometimes really about something bigger — differing ideas about roles, money, or whose family''s habits carry more weight in this house. Naming the bigger disagreement, away from the table, is usually more useful than re-litigating it over dinner. Worth knowing: If you and your partner land on different sides of a food decision, it''s worth raising it as a shared household question — not a referendum on whose upbringing was better.", "breakfast_a": "Rava dosa (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Different traditions, one table", "notes": "The way you were fed growing up and the way your partner was fed growing up were probably not identical, and neither tradition has to be declared the correct one for your shared table to work. Whichever household''s food habits feel more familiar to you, they were worked out over a long time for real reasons — that''s worth some respect, even from the one who didn''t grow up with them. It''s also fine for your own table to end up being its own mix, not a copy of either.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "You don''t need to agree on everything, just not undermine each other", "notes": "You and your partner don''t need identical food habits or opinions. What matters more is not undermining each other''s choices in front of family, and reserving a genuinely united, non-negotiable stance for anything involving safety rather than everyday preference. Worth knowing: If a disagreement about food or household roles ever becomes controlling, threatening, or makes you feel unsafe, that''s a different concern from an ordinary difference of opinion — speak with a doctor, counsellor, or a trusted support service rather than managing it alone.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-carrot toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a spoon of smooth peanut butter stirred in", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Making space for your own plate amid competing expectations", "notes": "Between a partner''s preferences, extended family''s expectations, and whatever''s easiest to cook for everyone else, it''s easy for your own plate to become an afterthought. Serving yourself a proper portion of something you actually chose is a small, real way of keeping your own eating in the picture.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-spinach curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Is there one meal this week where your own preference actually made it onto the table — and one where it quietly didn''t?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to treat a food disagreement at home as two people, or two families, trying to get the same thing right, rather than one side being wrong — while still making sure your own plate doesn''t get lost in the compromise?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: a calmer table matters more than a perfectly agreed-upon one, and your own plate belongs on it either way.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 66: Eating well when chai and snacking have replaced your own meals (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   66,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when chai and snacking have replaced your own meals',
   '"Chai and a biscuit between chores isn''t a meal — even when it''s most of what you''ve had by evening."',
   'Somewhere in an ordinary day of running a household, it''s easy to end up living mostly on chai, biscuits and whatever''s within reach, rather than sitting down to an actual meal. This isn''t a discipline problem. Regular meals are often the first thing to slip when a day is full of small, urgent tasks and no one else is prompting you to stop and eat. The trouble isn''t that chai or a snack itself is unhealthy — it''s when tea and biscuits displace enough nourishing food across the day that it often leaves you short on protein, iron and steady energy by evening, showing up as tiredness, irritability, or sudden hunger at dinner that has little to do with willpower. Smaller meals can nourish you too. The aim is enough food across the day, with protein, fibre-rich foods and other nutrient sources — not a particular plate size or way of eating. This week isn''t about judging how you eat; it''s about noticing where tea and biscuits have quietly become most of what you''ve had by evening, and closing that gap. This week doesn''t ask you to overhaul your day. It''s about making it a little easier to get enough real food in, however you eat it.',
   'If you monitor blood glucose: grazing on tea and biscuits rather than eating regular meals can make glucose harder to manage than a steadier pattern would. Choose unsweetened tea, pair it with a protein-rich bite rather than only biscuits, and keep meals on a regular schedule. The A and B meal options in this document aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which specific dishes fit your own targets. If you take levothyroxine: tea and its milk can reduce absorption if taken too close to your dose, the same way calcium-rich food can — keep your usual gap exactly as prescribed, and ask your pharmacist if you''re unsure how your routine should work. This is specific to levothyroxine; other thyroid medicines may have different instructions, so follow what your own prescription says. If you''re being treated for low iron: tea can also reduce how well an iron supplement is absorbed — follow your own treatment advice about tea timing, commonly a two-hour gap before or after a supplement such as ferrous sulfate. This doesn''t mean cutting out chai altogether, just keeping it away from your supplement by the interval you''ve been given. If you manage high blood pressure: packaged biscuits and salty snacks can add up across a day of frequent breaks — when practical, balance them with fruit, curd, or a handful of unsalted nuts. If you''re still breastfeeding: your own fluid and energy needs are higher right now — a day of tea breaks without a proper meal in between is worth deliberately avoiding, not just on the days it feels convenient. Chai has its place in the day — it just isn''t a substitute for the meals that keep you going.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs are valuable protein sources this week in particular, cooked fully. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; you don''t need six different dishes to get through the week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or uttapam is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. If you take levothyroxine, or you''re being treated for low iron, remember that timing matters as much as quantity — see Day 3. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Somewhere in an ordinary, full day, it''s easy to end up living mostly on chai, biscuits and whatever''s within reach — while an actual sit-down meal keeps getting pushed to \"after this next thing.\" Today''s just about noticing whether tea and biscuits have displaced enough nourishing food today. Smaller meals count too; the aim is enough nourishment across the day.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A cup of chai (200ml, made with 100ml milk) with a small bowl of curd (100g) and roasted chana (30g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Why grazing on tea and biscuits leaves you short by evening", "notes": "The trouble isn''t that chai or a snack is unhealthy on its own — it''s when tea and biscuits displace enough nourishing food across the day that it often leaves you short on protein and steady energy by evening, showing up as tiredness, irritability, or sudden hunger at dinner that has nothing to do with willpower. Smaller meals count too; the aim is enough nourishment across the day. Worth knowing: A cup of tea alongside food is fine; what matters is that tea and biscuits don''t quietly become most of what you eat across the day, however you eat it — standing at the counter or sitting down. Pairing chai with a protein-rich bite — roasted chana, a boiled egg, curd — rather than only biscuits is a small, realistic way to close that gap.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A cup of chai (200ml, made with 100ml milk) with a boiled egg and a small bowl of curd (100g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "If you take levothyroxine, or you''re treating low iron", "notes": "If you take levothyroxine, tea — including the milk in it — can reduce how much of the dose your body absorbs if the two are taken too close together, the same way calcium-rich food can. Tea, food and supplements don''t all follow one interchangeable timing rule — follow the specific gaps your own prescription and pharmacist have given you. Worth knowing: If this applies to you (you take levothyroxine): keep your dose at a consistent time, exactly as prescribed, and tell your doctor or pharmacist about any change in your tea, coffee or supplement habits — do not change the dose or timing on your own. If you''re being treated for low iron: follow your own treatment advice about tea timing — keep tea away from your iron supplement by the recommended interval (a two-hour gap before or after a supplement such as ferrous sulfate is commonly advised, though your prescriber''s instructions take priority). This doesn''t need to become a blanket ban on chai — just timing it away from the supplement.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A cup of chai (200ml, made with 100ml milk) with a small bowl of curd (100g) and roasted makhana (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Keeping food that nourishes you within easy reach", "notes": "A cup of tea takes minutes because it''s already a habit — having food that actually nourishes you can be just as quick, whether or not it''s eaten sitting down. A few ready components (cooked dal, boiled eggs, cut vegetables) mean a plate with real protein and nutrients on it is nearly as fast to put together as putting the kettle on.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A cup of chai (200ml, made with 100ml milk) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If you monitor blood glucose", "notes": "Grazing on tea and biscuits through the day, rather than eating regular meals, can make glucose harder to manage than a steadier meal pattern would — especially if the biscuits are sweetened and the gaps between meals stretch long. Choose meals and snacks that fit your individual glucose-management plan, including its guidance on portions and timing. Worth knowing: If this applies to you (you monitor blood glucose): choose unsweetened tea, pair it with a protein-rich bite rather than only biscuits, and keep your meals on a regular schedule rather than letting them slide later into the day. The A and B meal options in this document aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which specific dishes fit your own targets.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge (1 cup, with milk, 200ml, unsweetened) with a few crushed almonds", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong-masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-tomato curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An unsweetened cup of chai (200ml, made with 100ml milk) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with roasted peanuts (15g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Making room to eat enough across the day", "notes": "However you eat today — standing between tasks, in smaller portions through the day, or sitting down properly — the aim is enough food with protein, fibre-rich foods and other nutrient sources, not any particular plate size or way of eating. If a sit-down meal is realistic today, it''s worth choosing, but it isn''t the only version that counts.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal dosa (2 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A cup of chai (200ml, made with 100ml milk) with a small bowl of curd (100g) and roasted chana (30g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Notice whether tea and biscuits displaced enough nourishing food today. Smaller meals count too; the aim is enough nourishment across the day — is there one today that could include a bit more protein or a vegetable alongside it tomorrow?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A cup of chai (200ml, made with 100ml milk) with a small bowl of curd (100g) and roasted chana (30g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a cabbage sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to treat your own meals as appointments worth keeping, rather than something that happens if there''s time left over after the tea breaks?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve your own nourishment better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 67: Eating well as you wean your child off breastfeeding (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   67,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well as you wean your child off breastfeeding',
   '"Weaning gets to be gradual — for both of you."',
   'Weaning is often talked about as something that just needs to happen — dropped feeds, a bit of fussing, and then it''s done. In practice it''s a nutritional transition for your child and a physical and emotional one for you, and both go more smoothly slowly. This week is for mothers who have chosen — or need — to reduce or end breastfeeding. Continuing to breastfeed is equally valid. WHO supports breastfeeding alongside complementary foods for two years or beyond, for as long as it suits mother and child. As feeds drop, your child''s meals and snacks gradually take on more of that role — but this week''s focus stays on what the transition means for you. For you, milk production usually decreases as nursing or expressing becomes less frequent. Reducing feeds abruptly can increase the likelihood of uncomfortable fullness, engorgement and breast inflammation, while a gradual reduction often gives your body more time to adjust. Weaning can also carry mixed emotions, but significant mood symptoms should not automatically be attributed to hormones or to the speed of weaning. When circumstances allow, reduce one feed at a time and pause long enough for your breasts, your child and your routine to adjust comfortably before reducing another. Some people need only several days between changes; others need longer. Pain, marked fullness or distress is a reason to slow down and seek support — not a reason to force the schedule. Gradual reduction is supported, but there is no single mandatory timetable.',
   'If you notice fever, spreading redness, worsening breast pain, or feel acutely unwell as your supply eases: seek medical advice promptly rather than waiting to see if it settles — a qualified lactation professional can also help with feeding and milk-reduction technique. If weaning needs to happen quickly for a medical reason: such as a treatment or medication that isn''t compatible with breastfeeding, talk to your doctor or a lactation consultant about doing it safely — don''t delay care you need in order to keep breastfeeding. If you manage diabetes: maintain your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule through this transition, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. There''s no single right pace for weaning — only the one that keeps you both comfortable and well.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can contribute protein and micronutrients this week. Meat and eggs can provide iron, while varied lower-mercury fish can provide omega-3 fats. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed is completely fine; you don''t need a new dish every day. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti or paratha is one medium piece; an idli is 2-3 pieces, a dosa or cheela is 1-2 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially given the transition breastfeeding is going through right now, and more so if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Weaning — whether it unfolds over months or needs to happen quickly — is as much a change for your own body and routine as anything else this stage brings. This week is about your own nourishment through that transition.", "breakfast_a": "Semolina upma (1 cup cooked) with mixed vegetables and sambar, with a small bowl of curd (150g) on the side", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A serving of cut papaya with roasted cashews (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml) with a pinch of roasted jeera", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a tinda sabzi", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tinda sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Going slowly, on purpose", "notes": "Gradual reduction is often more comfortable than stopping abruptly, but there is no universal number of days that must pass between feeds. Reduce one feed at a time when circumstances allow, then pause until your breasts, your child and your routine feel reasonably settled before changing another. Some transitions unfold over weeks; others take months. There''s no fixed timeline you owe anyone — some manage the full transition in a few weeks, others take several months, and both are normal. Watching your own comfort, rather than a calendar, is a reasonable way to set the pace.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and sambar", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cabbage bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a capsicum sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a capsicum sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A kiwi with roasted pistachios (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of roasted chia seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup), a beans sabzi and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg, or a small bowl of roasted chana (30g) or curd (150g) if you''d rather keep it vegetarian"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "What changes in your own diet as feeds drop", "notes": "As milk production decreases, your energy needs may change. There is no fixed amount to subtract from your meals — keep regular, nourishing meals and adjust to your appetite, activity and health needs rather than automatically cutting food. Protein, iron and calcium needs don''t necessarily fall at the same rate as your energy needs do — it''s worth keeping an eye on all three individually through this transition, rather than assuming they move together.", "breakfast_a": "Oats idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a mushroom sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-mushroom curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A chikoo with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a pinch of turmeric", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-methi dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (2 eggs) with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Caring for your body as milk supply eases", "notes": "Your body needs time to adjust too. Reducing feeds gradually rather than stopping abruptly can lower the likelihood of painful fullness and breast inflammation, but it cannot guarantee that symptoms will not occur. Your comfort and any developing warning signs should guide the pace. If you become uncomfortably full, hand-express or pump only enough for comfort rather than trying to empty the breast completely. Use a cold compress for swelling and avoid forceful or deep breast massage. Worth knowing: If comfort measures aren''t helping and something feels off, don''t wait it out — see \"if a medical condition affects your meals\" above for when to seek help promptly. Cold cabbage leaves can remain an optional comfort measure, but they haven''t been shown to work better than an ordinary cold compress.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela (2 pieces) with mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-peas toast (2 eggs, 2 slices)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a peas sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a peas sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A mango with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a grated apple", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-pumpkin curry (3/4 cup) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-pumpkin curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If the timeline wasn''t yours to choose", "notes": "Not every weaning timeline is chosen freely — a medical treatment, an illness, or a return to work can all mean moving faster than you''d planned. If weaning needs to happen quickly for medical reasons, talk to your doctor or a lactation consultant about doing it safely; don''t delay treatment you need out of reluctance to stop breastfeeding. Weaning can bring mixed emotions, and some mothers notice changes in mood during the transition. The cause and intensity differ, so do not assume significant symptoms are simply hormonal or something you must wait out. For you: Speak with a healthcare professional if low mood, anxiety or loss of interest persists, interferes with daily life, or feels difficult to manage. Seek urgent help for thoughts of harming yourself or your child, severe confusion, feeling detached from reality, or hearing or seeing things others do not.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup cooked) with peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-bhindi toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a turai sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a turai sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of watermelon cubes with roasted sunflower seeds (1 tbsp) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of moong-sprout chaat (1 cup) made with sprouts cooked thoroughly until steaming hot, cooled safely before serving, with a squeeze of lemon and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a moong-tomato dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tomato sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg, or a small bowl of roasted chana (30g) or curd (150g) if you''d rather keep it vegetarian"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When everyone has an opinion about your timeline", "notes": "Weaning tends to invite unsolicited opinions — that you''re doing it too soon, too late, or the wrong way. None of that changes what your body actually needs through this transition, and you don''t owe anyone a defence of your own pace. If you''re weaning for a genuine health or medical reason, that reason is enough on its own — it doesn''t require an explanation to satisfy someone else''s comfort with your timeline.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with coriander chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-paneer bhurji (2 eggs, 40g paneer) with toast (2 slices)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beetroot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-beetroot curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of muskmelon with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml) with a few crushed curry leaves", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a drumstick sabzi", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a drumstick sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No new recipes today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Dinner B is swapped for a chicken option rather than repeating Day 1''s fish, so fish stays at three servings across the week rather than four. No forced choices today. If you''re partway through weaning, how has the pace felt so far — steady, rushed, or somewhere in between?", "breakfast_a": "Semolina upma (1 cup cooked) with mixed vegetables and sambar, with a small bowl of curd (150g) on the side", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A serving of cut papaya with roasted cashews (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml) with a pinch of roasted jeera", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a tinda sabzi", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a tinda sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to let this transition take as long as your own body actually needs, rather than the length of time you think it "should" take?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: small, steady adjustments serve you better than any single rule enforced all at once.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 68: Eating well when you're stuck in the same few meals yourself (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   68,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when you''re stuck in the same few meals yourself',
   '"A repetitive week of meals isn''t a habit to break — it''s often just where your energy is going right now."',
   'Somewhere in an ordinary stretch of motherhood, your own meals can narrow down to the same two or three combinations, repeated for days or weeks — not because you''ve stopped caring about food, but because deciding what to cook is one more decision you don''t have spare energy for right now. This is common, and it doesn''t need fixing on principle. A rotation of a few reliable meals that you actually eat regularly is doing its job, even if it looks repetitive from the outside. Repetition itself isn''t the concern — what matters is whether the meals you keep repeating cover enough protein, fibre and variety across a week, not whether they look varied on a plate. This week isn''t about forcing new recipes into your week. It''s about a few small, low-effort swaps that add variety without adding to your decision-making load.',
   'If you manage diabetes: repeating the same meals can actually make it easier to know your usual glucose response — mention any change to your usual rotation to your care team. The A and B meal options in this document aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which specific dishes fit your own targets. If your usual rotation is missing iron-rich food most days: that''s worth naming to your clinician rather than assuming it will average out — particularly if you notice fatigue, paleness or breathlessness that doesn''t match your sleep. If you''re still breastfeeding: your own fluid and energy needs continue regardless of how varied or repetitive your meals look this week. A repetitive week of meals doesn''t need to be treated as a habit to break — it just deserves a periodic, honest look at whether it''s still covering what you need.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, rotated for variety. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine; you don''t need six different dishes to get through the week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week, especially if you follow a restricted diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Somewhere in an ordinary stretch of motherhood, your own meals can narrow down to the same two or three combinations, repeated for days or weeks at a stretch — not because you''ve stopped caring about food, but because deciding what to cook is one more decision you don''t have spare energy for right now.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Why a repeated rotation is a reasonable way to eat", "notes": "A rotation of a few reliable meals that you actually eat regularly is doing its job, even if it looks repetitive from the outside. Repetition itself isn''t the concern — what matters is whether the meals you keep repeating cover enough protein, fibre and variety across a week, not whether they look varied on a plate.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of finely ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "One small swap, not a whole new menu", "notes": "You don''t need a new recipe to add variety — swapping the vegetable, the dal, or the protein inside a meal you already make on autopilot works just as well, and asks far less of you than learning something new. Worth knowing: Keeping the same format (grain, protein, vegetable) and just rotating what fills each slot is a realistic way to vary nutrients without adding decisions.", "breakfast_a": "Rava dosa (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If a repeated rotation is missing a food group", "notes": "A rotation that''s leaned on for weeks is worth a quick, honest look: does it include an iron-rich food most days, a dependable calcium source at more than one meal, and some vegetable or fruit variety across the week? If one of those has quietly dropped out, that''s more useful to notice than the repetition itself. Worth knowing: If your usual rotation is missing iron-rich food most days, or you notice fatigue, paleness or breathlessness that doesn''t match your sleep, mention it to your doctor rather than assuming it will average out over time.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "When repetition is doing real work for you", "notes": "Not having to decide what''s for dinner is a genuine form of rest during a demanding stretch. Leaning on a small rotation of meals you know will get eaten isn''t a failure of effort — it''s one less decision competing for energy you need elsewhere.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "One backup meal for the days nothing sounds good", "notes": "Having one simple meal you can make without thinking — the same dal, the same eggs, the same khichdi — is worth keeping in your back pocket for the days when even choosing between two options feels like too much.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Is there one meal from this week''s rotation you''d actually want to keep making regularly — not because you have to, but because it genuinely works for you?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to see a repetitive week of your own meals as a sign you''re conserving energy sensibly, rather than a habit you need to break?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: good-enough, sustainable meals matter more than varied ones, whatever this season of motherhood brings.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 69: Eating well on your own vegetarian diet (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   69,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well on your own vegetarian diet',
   '"A vegetarian diet can cover everything you need — it just asks you to be a little more deliberate about a few specific nutrients."',
   'These strategies focus on vegetarian eating, but the nutrients discussed matter for every mother — a vegetarian diet simply needs a little more deliberate attention to reach the same targets, since it excludes some of the foods that make these nutrients easiest to get. A vegetarian diet can fully meet your needs, including while breastfeeding. A small number of nutrients are worth some deliberate attention on this way of eating: iron, vitamin B12, vitamin D, iodine, zinc and calcium. None of this means your diet is inadequate — it means a few specific foods, pairings, or a supplement are worth including on purpose rather than by chance. These are not vegan menus: this week''s dishes include dairy and eggs alongside their plant-based options. Vegan readers should choose the plant-based dishes and replace dairy with suitable measured alternatives, with a reliable B12 source planned separately. This week walks through each nutrient, day by day.',
   'If you manage diabetes: the legume- and dairy-forward vegetarian meals here are generally steadier on glucose than refined-carbohydrate-heavy meals, but confirm portions and timing against your own care plan — the A and B options aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content. If you eat no animal products at all, including dairy and eggs: ask your doctor about a reliable vitamin B12 source — this is not optional to plan around on a strict plant-based diet. If you''re being treated for low iron: follow your prescriber''s guidance on supplement dose and timing in addition to, not instead of, the food pairings below. If you''re still breastfeeding: your calcium, vitamin D and B12 needs continue regardless of how long you''ve eaten this way. If you eat a vegetarian diet and feel unusually tired, breathless, or notice hair or skin changes, mention it to your doctor rather than assuming it''s simply part of the diet.',
   'This week is written for vegetarian-eating mothers, but every day below also includes a non-vegetarian option — nothing here requires you to change your diet. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd, milk or fortified soy milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk, paneer, or a fortified plant milk checked against the label for added calcium — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. Ragi and sesame can contribute, but should not be treated as equivalent replacements without checking quantities. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Iron, vitamin B12, iodine and vitamin D are worth particular attention across a full week on a vegetarian diet. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "If you eat a vegetarian diet yourself, a small number of nutrients are worth some deliberate attention: iron, vitamin B12, vitamin D, iodine, zinc and calcium. None of this means your diet is inadequate — it means a few specific foods or a supplement are worth including on purpose rather than by chance.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of fortified soy milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Getting enough iron from a vegetarian diet", "notes": "Plant iron is generally absorbed less efficiently than the haem iron in meat, poultry and seafood, but pairing iron-rich plant foods — lentils, chickpeas, dark leafy greens, tofu — with a source of vitamin C at the same meal meaningfully improves absorption. A squeeze of lemon over a dal, or a side of tomatoes or citrus fruit, is a simple, realistic habit. Worth knowing: Tea and coffee reduce iron absorption if taken close to an iron-rich meal — leave a gap of an hour or two either side where practical, especially if you have low iron.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a spinach-chana dal (3/4 cup) and a lemon wedge", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lemon wedge", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of fortified soy milk (200ml) with an orange", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a tomato-cucumber salad", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a tomato-cucumber salad", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg or a small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "If you don''t eat meat, fish or eggs at all", "notes": "This week''s menus are written for a vegetarian diet that includes dairy and eggs, not a vegan one. Vitamin B12 is found reliably only in animal foods, fortified foods, or a supplement — if you eat dairy and eggs, today''s meals already contribute some B12. If you follow a vegan diet, choose the plant-based dishes below and replace any dairy with a suitable measured alternative; ask your doctor about a reliable B12 source separately, since no amount of plant food alone provides dependable B12. Worth knowing: No amount of plant food alone provides dependable B12 — this isn''t optional to plan around if you eat no meat, fish, eggs or dairy.", "breakfast_a": "Rava dosa (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Egg curry with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A glass of fortified soy milk (200ml) with a handful of roasted almonds (15g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Iodine, zinc, and a few less-talked-about nutrients", "notes": "Iodine (from iodised salt, or dairy) and zinc (from legumes, nuts, seeds and dairy) are easy to overlook on a vegetarian diet. Using iodised salt within your usual salt allowance — not more salt in order to get more iodine — and including a legume or dairy source most days contributes to both, though it doesn''t by itself guarantee adequate intake.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of fortified soy milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Vitamin D and calcium together", "notes": "Vitamin D helps your body use the calcium you eat, but the two aren''t guaranteed by the same foods. Dairy is a reliable calcium source, but ordinary milk or curd doesn''t necessarily supply meaningful vitamin D unless it''s fortified — check the label rather than assuming. Sun exposure can contribute to vitamin D but isn''t a dependable guarantee of adequacy on its own. A vitamin D supplement is worth asking your doctor about, especially while breastfeeding.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of fortified soy milk (200ml) with a few crushed almonds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Building a reliable everyday rotation", "notes": "A vegetarian week that reliably includes a legume or dairy source most meals, iodised salt, and a vitamin-C pairing at iron-rich meals covers the bulk of what needs deliberate attention — the rest is a supplement conversation with your doctor, not a daily calculation.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd and a lemon wedge", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of fortified soy milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Which of this week''s nutrient notes — iron, B12, iodine, zinc, vitamin D, or calcium — is genuinely worth a conversation with your doctor for you specifically?", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of fortified soy milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to trust that your own vegetarian diet, with a few deliberate additions, is genuinely enough — rather than something to constantly double-check?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: your own nourishment deserves the same deliberate attention you''d give anyone else''s.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 70: Eating well when your own appetite doesn't match what you expect it to (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   70,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when your own appetite doesn''t match what you expect it to',
   '"Your appetite is information, not a schedule to enforce — some days it asks for more, some days less, and both can be normal."',
   'Your own appetite can shift week to week — sometimes dropping when you''re exhausted or stretched thin, sometimes surging if you''re breastfeeding or catching up on sleep debt. Neither direction is something to correct on principle. It''s worth noticing what your appetite is actually telling you before assuming it''s wrong. A day of eating less isn''t a problem to fix; a persistently low appetite over weeks is worth a conversation with your doctor — sooner than that if your intake has dropped substantially or you feel unwell. This week walks through both directions — a dropped appetite and a surging one — and when either is worth a closer look.',
   'If you manage diabetes: an unusually low or high appetite can affect your glucose pattern — follow your own diabetes management plan''s guidance when your appetite changes noticeably, and mention the change to your care team. The A and B meal options in this document aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content, so check which specific dishes fit your own targets. If you''re recovering from a delivery complication or surgery: a temporarily reduced appetite is common during recovery — smaller, more frequent meals can be easier to manage than three full plates. If you''re still breastfeeding: a persistently low appetite is still worth raising with your doctor even though hunger often runs higher during this stage. A low appetite that persists for more than a couple of weeks, or comes with low mood or disinterest in things you''d usually enjoy, deserves attention rather than being waited out.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite, and it''s equally fine to eat less than what''s listed on a lower-appetite day. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Your own appetite can shift week to week — sometimes dropping when you''re exhausted or stretched thin, sometimes surging if you''re breastfeeding or catching up on sleep debt. Neither direction is something to correct on principle; it''s worth noticing what your appetite is actually telling you before assuming it''s wrong.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "When your appetite has quietly dropped", "notes": "If you''ve noticed you''re eating less than usual — skipping meals without much hunger, or stopping halfway through a plate — it''s worth asking whether stress, exhaustion, or simply not having a moment to sit down is behind it, rather than assuming your body needs less. Smaller, more frequent meals can be easier to get through than three full plates if a low appetite has set in. Worth knowing: If a low appetite has lasted more than a couple of weeks, or comes with low mood, disinterest in things you''d usually enjoy, or difficulty caring for yourself, mention it to your doctor — this is common and treatable, not a personal failing. If your intake has dropped substantially, or you feel unwell, don''t wait that long — contact your doctor sooner.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of finely ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "When your appetite has surged instead", "notes": "Hunger can increase while breastfeeding — your body is asking for more energy, and eating in response, including extra snacks between meals, is appropriate rather than something to hold back on. That said, a marked, persistent or unexplained change in appetite still deserves attention rather than being assumed to be routine.", "breakfast_a": "Rava dosa (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) or a glass of milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Eating on a schedule that isn''t your own", "notes": "A packed day of caregiving can mean your own meals get pushed later and later, or skipped when the moment passes. Keeping something quick and ready — boiled eggs, curd, fruit, roasted nuts — means an interrupted meal doesn''t have to mean no meal.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Trusting fullness as much as hunger", "notes": "Just as it''s fine to eat less on a day your appetite is genuinely lower, it''s equally fine to stop eating when you''re full, even if a plate isn''t finished — neither direction needs to be forced against what your body is actually telling you today.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When a low appetite needs more than patience", "notes": "A day or two of eating less is usually nothing to worry about. A drop in appetite that persists for weeks, or comes with noticeable weight loss, fatigue, or low mood, is worth raising with your doctor rather than waiting to see if it passes on its own. If your intake has dropped substantially, or you feel unwell, seek help sooner than that — don''t wait out a couple of weeks first. Worth knowing: This applies whether or not you''re breastfeeding — a persistently low appetite deserves attention regardless of feeding stage. If you manage diabetes, follow your own diabetes plan''s guidance for appetite changes as well.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Looking back over the week, did your appetite mostly match what you expected, or did it surprise you — and either way, did you feel able to follow it rather than override it?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to treat your own appetite as reliable information about what you need right now, rather than something to second-guess against a schedule?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: your own hunger and fullness deserve to be trusted and responded to.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 71: Eating well with your own food allergy or intolerance (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   71,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well with your own food allergy or intolerance',
   '"Managing a food allergy well isn''t about eating less — it''s about knowing your safe substitutions well enough that avoidance doesn''t cost you nourishment."',
   'This week''s guidance applies if you have a diagnosed food allergy or intolerance yourself. If you don''t, none of the allergen-avoidance notes below need to apply to you — continue with the regular maternal meal options elsewhere in this series. Managing a food allergy well isn''t about eating less — it''s about knowing your safe substitutions well enough that avoidance doesn''t cost you nourishment. Your own ingredient checks, safe preparation and prescribed allergy plan deserve consistent attention. This week walks through cross-contact precautions, nutrient substitutions, and eating safely outside your own kitchen — all for your own allergy.',
   'If your allergy carries a risk of anaphylaxis: keep your own prescribed epinephrine auto-injector accessible to you and check its expiry date periodically. For suspected anaphylaxis, use your auto-injector immediately and call local emergency services, following your own emergency action plan. If you avoid dairy for allergy or intolerance reasons: replace its calcium with a measured, label-checked fortified alternative or calcium-set tofu (if soy is tolerated) rather than assuming other foods make up the gap by default, and check the alternative''s protein content too, not just its calcium. If your trigger is milk protein rather than lactose: lactose-free dairy is not a safe substitute — it still contains the allergenic proteins. You need a genuinely dairy-free alternative. If you manage diabetes alongside a food allergy: check both the allergen content and the carbohydrate content of any substitution before relying on it regularly. If you''re still breastfeeding: ask your doctor whether any of your own allergy avoidance needs adjustment for this stage — most don''t, but it''s worth confirming. If your allergy list is broad enough that planning nutritionally adequate meals feels genuinely difficult on your own, ask your doctor for a referral to a dietitian familiar with food allergy management.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs appear as options below for mothers whose allergy doesn''t involve them. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals, and neither option is written to be safe for every allergy. Check every ingredient in both the A and B option against your own trigger foods before choosing — dairy, for instance, appears in most slots across this week in both options, so the second listed option is not automatically the safe one just because it differs from the first. If neither option is safe for your specific allergy on a given day, substitute your own checked, familiar dish for that meal rather than choosing the less-unsafe option. Repeating a combination you already know is safe and enjoyable is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g) — skip these if nuts or seeds are among your trigger foods. The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer, or a measured fortified alternative if dairy is your trigger food — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "If you manage a food allergy or intolerance yourself, today''s about taking stock: is your current diet reliably avoiding the trigger food, and does it still cover the protein, calcium and other nutrients that food would otherwise have contributed?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Making label-checking a habit for your own meals", "notes": "Your own ingredient checks, safe preparation and prescribed allergy plan deserve the same consistent attention any serious health need requires. If you share a kitchen or eat leftovers from family cooking, the same cross-contact precautions that would protect anyone with a serious allergy apply to protecting you. Worth knowing: If your allergy carries a risk of anaphylaxis, keep your own prescribed epinephrine auto-injector accessible to you and check its expiry date periodically. For suspected anaphylaxis, use your auto-injector immediately and call local emergency services, following your own emergency action plan.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of finely ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg (if eggs aren''t your trigger food)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Replacing what an avoided food would have given you", "notes": "If you avoid dairy, replace its calcium with a measured, label-checked fortified alternative or calcium-set tofu (if soy is tolerated) rather than assuming other foods make up the gap by default — check the alternative''s protein content too, since a similar calcium figure doesn''t mean it matches dairy''s protein contribution. If you avoid nuts, seeds and legumes can often fill a similar role for healthy fats and protein — check with your doctor if your allergy list is broad enough that this feels complicated to plan around alone. Worth knowing: A milk allergy and lactose intolerance are different things: lactose-free milk or curd can help with lactose intolerance, but it still contains the same allergenic proteins, so it isn''t a safe substitute if milk protein itself is your trigger.", "breakfast_a": "Rava dosa (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g, or a calcium-fortified alternative if dairy is your trigger food)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml, or a calcium-fortified alternative)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with a small bowl of curd (150g, or a calcium-fortified alternative)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds (or a calcium-fortified alternative)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer, or calcium-set tofu if soy is tolerated and dairy is your trigger food; check the label for calcium and protein) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) or a calcium-fortified alternative"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Cooking once, safely, for the whole household", "notes": "If your allergy differs from the rest of your household''s needs, cook a shared base dish, set aside your own portion using a clean utensil, and only then add your trigger ingredient to the rest for everyone else — rather than cooking two entirely separate meals from scratch. Adding the allergen separately at the end doesn''t by itself make the shared dish safe for you unless your portion was fully separated first. Worth knowing: Keep a separate utensil, cutting board or pan for your allergen-free cooking if cross-contact is a meaningful risk for you, and wash shared surfaces thoroughly between uses, following your own allergy plan''s precautions.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Eating out or accepting food from others", "notes": "Restaurant meals and food prepared by others carry the highest cross-contact risk for a serious allergy. It''s entirely reasonable to ask direct questions about ingredients and preparation, choose simpler dishes with fewer hidden ingredients, or decline food you can''t confirm is safe — none of that is an overreaction.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "A familiar backup meal, made safely", "notes": "Keeping one familiar meal made with checked ingredients and safe preparation in mind — something you''ve made this way before and are confident about — is worth having for the days when checking every label feels like too much on top of everything else.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you, keeping your own allergen checks in mind. Is there one substitution from this week that you''d like to keep using regularly, now that you''ve tried it?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to give your own food allergy the same careful, matter-of-fact attention any serious health need deserves — without it feeling like an extra burden on top of everything else?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: managing your own health needs carefully is part of nourishing yourself, not separate from it.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 72: Eating well when you're constipated yourself (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   72,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when you''re constipated yourself',
   '"Postpartum constipation is extremely common and rarely about willpower — fibre, fluids, and time usually help more than they get credit for."',
   'Postpartum constipation can persist or recur well beyond the earliest weeks — diet, fluid intake, medicines such as iron supplements, hormonal shifts, and reduced movement are the main ongoing contributors at this stage. It isn''t a sign you''re doing something wrong, and it usually responds well to a few specific, manageable changes. This week focuses on fibre, fluids, and a gentle, non-shameful approach to something that affects a large share of postpartum mothers, whether or not anyone talks about it openly. This week''s menus introduce several high-fibre foods together — if your usual diet has been lower in fibre, increase it gradually over a week or two rather than switching everything at once, to reduce bloating or gas as your gut adjusts. If this isn''t something you''re currently dealing with, this week''s fibre-and-fluid habits are still reasonable general practice for most mothers — though if you follow a prescribed fluid restriction for a heart, kidney or other condition, follow your prescriber''s fluid guidance instead of the amounts suggested here.',
   'If you''re taking an iron supplement: don''t stop it without talking to your doctor first — ask instead about formulation, dose timing, or a stool softener alongside it, and keep fibre and fluids up on days you take it. If you manage diabetes: a higher-fibre pattern is generally supportive, but confirm portions against your own care plan — the A and B meal options here aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content. If you follow a prescribed fluid restriction: for a heart, kidney or other condition, follow your prescriber''s guidance on fluid intake rather than the general amounts suggested here, and ask what fibre approach is safe within your restriction. If bowel movements are persistently painful, incomplete, or require significant straining: ask your doctor about a pelvic floor assessment — pelvic floor dysfunction is a common, treatable contributor to constipation. If you''re still breastfeeding: your fluid needs are higher than usual on top of what constipation relief requires — increase water intake accordingly, within any prescribed fluid restriction that applies to you. Constipation that persists beyond a couple of weeks despite these changes is worth a routine visit to your doctor. Severe or constant abdominal pain, vomiting, being completely unable to pass gas, or blood in your stool are different — these need prompt medical assessment regardless of how long the constipation has lasted, not a wait-and-see approach.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here — pairing them with a fibre-rich vegetable or whole grain side keeps the week''s fibre focus intact. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice, brown rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa, chilla or uttapam is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Postpartum constipation can persist or recur well beyond the earliest weeks — diet, fluid intake, medicines such as iron supplements, hormonal shifts, and reduced movement are the main ongoing contributors at this stage. It isn''t a sign you''re doing something wrong. Worth knowing: This week''s menu introduces several high-fibre foods at once. If your usual diet has been lower in fibre, increase it gradually over a week or two rather than switching everything at once — a sudden jump can cause bloating or gas as your gut adjusts.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge (1 cup, made with milk) with a chopped apple and flaxseed (1 tbsp)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, whole-grain, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Brown rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a sautéed spinach sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with soaked chia seeds (1 tbsp)", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a beetroot sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with whole-wheat roti (2 pieces) and a beetroot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few prunes"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Fluids matter as much as fibre", "notes": "Adding fibre without enough fluid can make constipation worse, not better. Aim for water steadily through the day rather than all at once, and consider starting the morning with a large glass of water before anything else. Worth knowing: If you''re breastfeeding, your fluid needs are higher than usual — drink enough that your urine runs pale, and more on hot days or after activity. If you follow a prescribed fluid restriction for a heart, kidney or other condition, follow your prescriber''s guidance on fluid intake instead of the general amounts suggested here.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with whole-wheat toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Brown rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a bhindi sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a bhindi sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of coconut water (200ml) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti (2 pieces) with a lauki-chana curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with whole-wheat roti (2 pieces) and a bhindi sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm water with a few soaked prunes"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "If iron supplements are part of the picture", "notes": "Iron supplements are a common cause of constipation. Don''t stop them without talking to your doctor first — instead, ask whether a different formulation, a different dosing schedule recommended by your prescriber, or a stool softener alongside it might help, and keep fibre and fluids up on days you take it. Worth knowing: Taking an iron supplement with food can ease stomach discomfort, though it may reduce absorption slightly — ask your prescriber what timing works best for you.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge (1 cup, made with milk) with soaked raisins", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, whole-grain, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Brown rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage-carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage-carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted flaxseed (1 tbsp) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few chopped figs", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (75g paneer) with whole-wheat roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few soaked prunes"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If bowel movements are persistently painful or difficult", "notes": "If bowel movements are consistently painful, require significant straining, or feel incomplete, that''s worth assessing rather than assuming it''s normal. Pelvic floor dysfunction is a common, treatable contributor to constipation — it''s not something you need to just live with, and a physiotherapist who specialises in pelvic health can help. Worth knowing: If painful or difficult bowel movements continue despite fibre, fluids and gentle movement, ask your doctor whether a pelvic floor assessment or referral would help.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma made with broken wheat (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, whole-grain, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Brown rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted pumpkin seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few soaked prunes", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with whole-wheat roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Gentle movement helps too", "notes": "A short, gentle walk can help move things along alongside diet changes — this isn''t about exercising for weight, just encouraging normal digestion.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam made with mixed grains (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with whole-wheat toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Brown rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton) with brown rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with whole-wheat roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few soaked prunes"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When it''s more than a passing issue", "notes": "If constipation persists beyond a couple of weeks despite fibre, fluids and gentle movement, contact your doctor rather than waiting it out — this is common enough that your care team will have seen it many times before. Worth knowing: Some symptoms need prompt medical assessment regardless of how long the constipation has lasted, not a wait-and-see couple of weeks: severe or constant abdominal pain, vomiting, being completely unable to pass gas, or blood in your stool.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, whole-grain, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi made with brown rice (1 heaped cup) with curd and a side of sautéed greens", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a few soaked prunes", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with whole-wheat roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Did increasing fibre and fluids this week make a noticeable difference — and is there one change from this week (a glass of water first thing, prunes as a snack) worth keeping as a regular habit?", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge (1 cup, made with milk) with a chopped apple and flaxseed (1 tbsp)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, whole-grain, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Brown rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a sautéed spinach sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with brown rice (1 cup cooked) and a sautéed spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with soaked chia seeds (1 tbsp)", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a beetroot sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with whole-wheat roti (2 pieces) and a beetroot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few prunes"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to treat a common, ordinary postpartum issue like this one as worth mentioning to your doctor or a friend, rather than something to manage quietly on your own?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: your own body''s ordinary, common experiences deserve attention and care too.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 73: Eating well when a toothache or gum ache gets in the way (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   73,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when a toothache or gum ache gets in the way',
   '"A few days of tooth or gum discomfort don''t need to shrink your meals to nothing — soft, cool and gentle stand in just fine until it settles."',
   'Tooth or gum pain can make ordinary meals feel like more trouble than they''re worth. You don''t need to skip meals or push through pain to eat well this week — soft, cool, gentle foods can carry you through a flare just as well as your usual plate. Mild sensitivity for a day or two can happen on its own, but toothache lasting more than two days is worth a dentist''s assessment rather than something to keep managing at home. That''s a different matter from an emergency — difficulty breathing or swallowing needs urgent medical care right away, while persistent pain or visible swelling calls for a prompt dentist''s appointment. This week focuses on comfortable, nutritious eating during a flare, alongside — never instead of — professional dental care when it''s needed.',
   'If you manage diabetes: soft, well-cooked meals here can still fit your glucose plan — confirm portions and timing against it, since the A and B options aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content. If pain is affecting how much you''re eating or drinking: prioritise fluids and small, frequent soft meals over forcing a full plate, and see a dentist sooner if this continues beyond a few days. If you''re still breastfeeding: ask your doctor, pharmacist or dentist which pain relief option is suitable for you specifically — don''t assume a general recommendation applies to your situation without checking. Difficulty breathing or swallowing needs emergency medical care immediately. Facial swelling, fever, difficulty opening your mouth, or pain spreading toward your ear or neck need a prompt dental or medical appointment rather than a wait-and-see approach.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here, cooked tender and soft during a flare. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Tooth or gum pain can make ordinary meals feel like more trouble than they''re worth. You don''t need to skip meals or push through pain to eat well this week — soft, cool, gentle foods can carry you through a flare just as well as your usual plate.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (1 cup, soft-cooked) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Scrambled eggs (2 eggs, tender but fully cooked) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked, soft) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a well-cooked pumpkin sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken, tender-cooked) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a well-cooked pumpkin sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A ripe banana with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk at a comfortable temperature (200ml, made with 100g curd)", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi (1 heaped cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer, soft-cooked) with soft khichdi (1 heaped cup)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "When it''s ordinary sensitivity, and when it isn''t", "notes": "A day or two of mild tooth sensitivity or tender gums can happen on its own, but toothache that lasts more than two days is worth a dentist''s assessment rather than something to keep managing at home — go ahead and book that appointment now rather than waiting to see if it passes. Worth knowing: This is different from an emergency: difficulty breathing or swallowing needs urgent medical care right away. Severe pain, or pain lasting more than two days, calls for a prompt dentist''s appointment rather than an emergency room, but it still shouldn''t wait indefinitely.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces, soft) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs, tender but fully cooked) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a well-cooked bottle gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish, tender but fully cooked) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a well-cooked bottle gourd sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A ripe mango or papaya with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a soft banana", "dinner_a": "Soft dalia khichdi (1 heaped cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (tender but fully cooked) with soft dalia khichdi (1 heaped cup)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Adjusting texture and temperature for a few days", "notes": "Avoiding very hot, very cold, hard, sticky or crunchy foods for a few days can make eating much more comfortable during a flare, and chewing on the unaffected side helps too. This is a short-term adjustment — your usual textures can come straight back once it settles.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma made with soft-cooked semolina (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Soft paneer bhurji (75g paneer) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked, soft) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a well-cooked carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken, tender-cooked) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a well-cooked carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A soft ripe pear with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml), lukewarm rather than hot or iced", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi (1 heaped cup) with a well-cooked spinach sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (75g paneer, soft-cooked) with soft khichdi (1 heaped cup)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Gum sensitivity and your own nutrition", "notes": "Hormonal changes in pregnancy and postpartum can make gums more sensitive for some mothers — this is a hormonal effect, not a sign your calcium is being used up. Vitamin-C-rich foods and a steady calcium intake support healthy gums generally, but they''re a support alongside dental care, not a substitute for it — routine dental checkups are still worth keeping up postpartum.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup, soft) with orange segments and a small bowl of curd (150g) — swap the orange for a soft ripe banana if citrus stings", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, tender but fully cooked, soft crust) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a well-cooked tomato-based sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken-and-tomato curry (100g chicken, tender-cooked) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava or orange with a small bowl of curd (150g) — swap for a soft ripe pear or banana if citrus or guava stings", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a soft mandarin — swap for a soft banana if citrus stings", "dinner_a": "Roti (soft, 2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer, soft-cooked) with soft roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Pain relief that''s actually safe for you right now", "notes": "A warm salt-water rinse is a simple, safe comfort measure you can use anytime. For pain relief beyond that, ask your doctor, pharmacist or dentist which option is suitable for you while breastfeeding, and use it exactly as directed — this isn''t a good week to self-medicate based on guesswork. Worth knowing: Ask specifically about your own situation — your prescriber can confirm what''s suitable for you while breastfeeding, and dosing that''s fine for someone else may not be right for you.", "breakfast_a": "Rava dosa (soft-cooked, 2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, soft crust) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a well-cooked beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish, tender but fully cooked) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a well-cooked beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A soft banana with roasted-then-ground nut butter (1 tbsp) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with soaked, softened dates — swap for a soft ripe banana if sticky textures pull at sensitive teeth", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi (1 heaped cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (tender but fully cooked) with soft khichdi (1 heaped cup)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When to get help sooner rather than later", "notes": "Facial swelling, a fever, difficulty opening your mouth fully, pain spreading toward your ear or neck, or a bad taste alongside the pain can point to an infection or abscess — these need a prompt dental or medical appointment, not a wait-and-see approach. Worth knowing: Difficulty breathing or swallowing is an emergency — get medical help immediately rather than waiting for a dental appointment. Spreading swelling or a fever alongside dental pain still needs prompt care, though not necessarily an emergency room.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (soft-cooked, 2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, soft crust) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup, soft) with curd", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish, tender but fully cooked) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a well-cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A soft mandarin with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a soft banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (soft, 2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup, well-cooked)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer, soft-cooked) with soft roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. If the discomfort hasn''t settled by now, is a dentist''s visit something you can book this week rather than continue waiting out?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (1 cup, soft-cooked) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Scrambled eggs (2 eggs, tender but fully cooked) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked, soft) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a well-cooked pumpkin sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken, tender-cooked) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a well-cooked pumpkin sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A ripe banana with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk at a comfortable temperature (200ml, made with 100g curd)", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi (1 heaped cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer, soft-cooked) with soft khichdi (1 heaped cup)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to treat your own tooth or gum pain as worth prompt attention, the same way you''d encourage anyone else to get it checked?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: your own body''s discomfort deserves care and attention, not just working around it.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 74: Supporting your own iron levels postpartum (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   74,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Supporting your own iron levels postpartum',
   '"Iron-rich food supports you either way — but confirmed low iron needs your own doctor-led testing and treatment plan, not guesswork."',
   'Iron needs after birth depend on your own iron stores, blood loss at delivery, whether your periods have returned, and any current pregnancy — not on breastfeeding by itself. Follow your own testing and treatment advice rather than assuming breastfeeding means you need extra iron. This plan supports healthy iron intake and can sit alongside treatment, but it doesn''t treat confirmed iron deficiency by itself. Low iron often causes no obvious symptoms, which is why a blood test — not symptom-watching — is what actually confirms it. If a test shows you''re low, follow the supplement dose and follow-up testing your own doctor prescribes. This week walks through heme vs. non-heme iron, vitamin C pairing, and what''s actually worth asking your doctor about.',
   'If you''re prescribed an iron supplement: take it as your own doctor directs — dose, timing and duration are their call based on your test results, not a general rule. If you manage diabetes: the legume- and vegetable-forward meals here are generally steadier on glucose, but confirm portions and timing against your own care plan — the A and B options aren''t matched for exact carbohydrate content. If you follow a vegetarian diet: lean on iron-fortified cereal, lentils, beans, tofu and dark leafy greens paired with vitamin C foods, since you''re relying entirely on non-heme sources. If you''re still breastfeeding: breastfeeding on its own doesn''t automatically mean higher iron needs — what actually matters is your own iron stores, bleeding history and whether periods have returned. A persistently low appetite or fatigue is still worth raising with your doctor regardless. If you feel unusually tired, breathless, pale, or notice hair or nail changes, ask your doctor for iron testing rather than assuming it''s simply part of postpartum life.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here — meat and fish contribute more readily absorbed heme iron, while eggs add protein and some non-heme iron. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Iron needs after birth depend on your own iron stores, blood loss at delivery, whether your periods have returned, and any current pregnancy — not on breastfeeding by itself. This plan supports healthy iron intake and can sit alongside treatment, but it doesn''t treat confirmed iron deficiency by itself — a blood test is what actually confirms it. Follow your own testing and treatment advice rather than assuming breastfeeding alone means you need extra iron.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a lemon wedge", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lemon wedge", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with an orange", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach dal (3/4 cup) and a tomato side", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a tomato side", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Heme and non-heme iron, and why the difference matters", "notes": "Heme iron — from meat, poultry and fish — is absorbed more readily than the non-heme iron in lentils, beans, greens and fortified cereal. Eggs contribute iron and protein too, but their iron is non-heme, not equivalent to meat''s for absorption. Pairing non-heme iron sources with a vitamin C food at the same meal — citrus, tomato, bell pepper, strawberries — meaningfully improves how much you actually absorb, and it''s an easy habit rather than something to measure precisely.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a spinach-chana dal (3/4 cup) and a lemon wedge", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lemon wedge", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a tomato-cucumber salad", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a tomato-cucumber salad", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg or a small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "What actually confirms low iron", "notes": "Low iron often causes no obvious symptoms at all, which is why a blood test — not symptom-watching — is what actually confirms it. If you''ve been feeling unusually tired, breathless, pale, or notice hair or nail changes, it''s worth asking your doctor for iron testing rather than assuming it will resolve on its own. Worth knowing: Postpartum anaemia is common enough that your doctor will likely already consider it — ask directly if it hasn''t come up, especially if you had significant blood loss at delivery.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "If you''re prescribed an iron supplement", "notes": "Any iron supplement should come from your own doctor, based on your own test results — not started on your own or borrowed from someone else''s prescription. Taking it consistently, at the timing and dose your prescriber recommends, matters more than any single day''s food choices. Worth knowing: Iron supplements are commonly advised with a roughly two-hour gap from tea or coffee, which reduce absorption — but follow your own prescriber''s specific instructions over any general rule.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup) and a lemon wedge", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lemon wedge", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with an orange", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If your own diet is vegetarian", "notes": "Include plain, iron-fortified cereal (check the label for fortification level), lentils, beans, tofu and dark leafy greens regularly, and pair them with a vitamin C food at the same meal. Eggs, if you eat them, add further iron and protein; dairy can stay part of your diet but shouldn''t be relied on as an iron source, since it isn''t one.", "breakfast_a": "Rava dosa (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of fortified soy milk (200ml) with an orange", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Building an iron-aware rotation without overthinking it", "notes": "An iron-rich food at most meals, paired with a vitamin C food where it''s easy, and tea or coffee kept away from those meals — this covers the bulk of what matters day to day. The rest, if a test shows you''re actually low, is a conversation with your doctor about supplementation and follow-up testing.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd and a lemon wedge", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Is getting an iron test — if you haven''t had one since delivery — something worth asking your doctor about at your next visit?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a lemon wedge", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a lemon wedge", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with an orange", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach dal (3/4 cup) and a tomato side", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-spinach curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a tomato side", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to treat fatigue or low energy as a fair question to ask your doctor about, rather than something to assume is just part of having a young child?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: your own body''s needs deserve real attention, tested and treated properly rather than guessed at.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 75: Eating well when you have a stomach bug yourself (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   75,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when you have a stomach bug yourself',
   '"Small, frequent fluids and food as you can manage it support your hydration and a steady return to normal eating — not fasting or a restrictive diet."',
   'Most stomach bugs are viral, self-limited, and pass within about a week. With an uncomplicated bug, dehydration is usually the main immediate concern — small, frequent fluids matter more right now than a full meal. Current guidance has moved away from a restrictive, bland-only diet — continuing your normal foods in whatever amount you can manage, once you''re keeping fluids down, supports hydration and a return to eating normally, rather than staying on a limited menu longer than necessary. This week walks through hydration, a gradual return to your usual meals, and the specific symptoms that mean it''s time to call your doctor. Wash your hands with soap and water often, especially after using the bathroom and before handling food, and avoid preparing food for others until at least 48 hours after vomiting or diarrhoea has stopped — this protects your household while you''re contagious.',
   'If you manage diabetes: reduced intake and illness can both affect your glucose pattern — follow your own diabetes plan''s guidance for sick days, and contact your care team if you''re struggling to eat or drink normally. If you''re still breastfeeding: most gastroenteritis doesn''t require stopping — check with your doctor if you''re unsure, and keep your own fluids up to support your supply. If you''re on any regular medication: check with your doctor or pharmacist whether vomiting or diarrhoea affects how well it''s absorbed, and whether any adjustment is needed. Blood in your stool or vomit, severe or localised abdominal pain, being unable to keep any fluids down, or worsening rather than easing symptoms need prompt medical attention, not a wait-and-see approach.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can return to your meals once you''re tolerating regular food again — there''s no need to avoid them specifically. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite and how your stomach feels. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Most stomach bugs are viral, short-lived, and pass within about a week. Dehydration is usually the main immediate concern, not lack of food — small sips of fluid, more often than feels natural, matter more right now than getting a full meal in. Hydration alongside the menu: if you have vomiting, frequent watery stools or feel light-headed, sip an oral rehydration solution (ORS) frequently in small amounts, following the product''s instructions — mix a sachet with exactly the water quantity stated on the packet, never stronger or weaker, and never add extra sugar or salt. ORS replaces lost fluid and electrolytes; it doesn''t replace meals once you''re able to eat again. Worth knowing: While you''re actively vomiting or can''t keep food down, hydration and whatever you can tolerate matter far more than meeting the usual twice-daily calcium pattern below — that catches up naturally once eating settles.", "breakfast_a": "Plain toast (2 slices) with a small bowl of curd (150g), once you can keep food down", "breakfast_b": "A hard-boiled egg, fully cooked, with plain toast (1 slice) and a glass of milk (200ml), once you can keep food down", "lunch_a": "Plain khichdi (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_b": "Plain khichdi (1 cup) with a boiled egg on the side", "nourishment_break_a": "A ripe banana with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd)", "dinner_a": "Plain rice (1 cup cooked) with a thin moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Plain rice (1 cup cooked) with a thin moong dal (3/4 cup) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g), once tolerated"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "You don''t need to fast, once you can keep fluids down", "notes": "The old advice to eat only bland, restrictive foods during a stomach bug has changed — current guidance favours returning to your normal diet, in whatever amount you can manage, once you''re keeping fluids down, rather than staying on a limited menu longer than necessary. This supports hydration and a return to eating normally, rather than a promise of a faster recovery on its own. Milk and dairy don''t usually need routine avoiding either, though some people find their gut briefly handles lactose less well for a week or two after a bug — if milk seems to be making things worse, swapping to a lactose-free milk or curd, or a plain dairy-free alternative, for a few days is a reasonable adjustment rather than something to push through.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup, mild spice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs, mild spice) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup, mild spice) and a well-cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken, mild spice) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a well-cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A ripe banana with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup, mild spice)", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer, mild spice) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Easing back toward your usual meals", "notes": "As you feel steadier, moving back toward your normal variety of foods — rather than staying on plain toast and rice longer than you need to — supports a fuller recovery. There''s no fixed schedule here; let your own appetite and how your stomach feels guide the pace.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with a mild sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a mild cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish, mild spice) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mild cauliflower sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (mild spice) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Skip the self-medicating", "notes": "Anti-diarrhoeal medicines and anti-nausea medicines aren''t something to start on your own — check with your doctor first, especially while breastfeeding. Most stomach bugs resolve with fluids and time rather than medication. Worth knowing: If you''re breastfeeding, most cases of gastroenteritis don''t require stopping — check with your doctor if you''re unsure, and keep your own fluid intake up to support your milk supply.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma (1 cup, mild spice) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a mild beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken, mild spice) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a mild beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd)", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup, mild spice)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer, mild spice) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Rebuilding what a stomach bug can deplete", "notes": "A few days of reduced intake, plus fluid loss, can leave you depleted even after the bug itself has passed. Getting back to regular meals with protein, fibre and fluids helps you recover your energy, not just settle your stomach.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When it needs more than fluids and time", "notes": "Blood in your stool or vomit, severe or localised abdominal pain, being unable to keep any fluids down at all, or symptoms that keep worsening rather than easing are signs to call your doctor promptly, not signs to wait out. Worth knowing: Seek urgent care if you can''t keep any fluids down, feel confused or unusually drowsy, or notice signs of significant dehydration such as a very dry mouth, little or no urination, or dizziness on standing.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or, if you''re feeling steadier, swap in whichever meals from later in the week suit your appetite better. Are you back to your normal appetite and energy by now — and if not, is that worth mentioning to your doctor rather than waiting further?", "breakfast_a": "Plain toast (2 slices) with a small bowl of curd (150g), once you can keep food down", "breakfast_b": "A hard-boiled egg, fully cooked, with plain toast (1 slice) and a glass of milk (200ml), once you can keep food down", "lunch_a": "Plain khichdi (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "lunch_b": "Plain khichdi (1 cup) with a boiled egg on the side", "nourishment_break_a": "A ripe banana with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd)", "dinner_a": "Plain rice (1 cup cooked) with a thin moong dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Plain rice (1 cup cooked) with a thin moong dal (3/4 cup) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g), once tolerated"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to let yourself recover at whatever pace your body needs this week, without pressure to bounce back to normal meals faster than feels right?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: your own recovery from illness deserves patience and real care, not rushing.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 76: Eating well when you're taking a new medication while breastfeeding (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   76,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when you''re taking a new medication while breastfeeding',
   '"Most medicines are compatible with breastfeeding — the way to find out about yours is to ask, not to guess or stop feeding just in case."',
   'If you''re currently breastfeeding: being prescribed or considering a new medicine doesn''t automatically mean you need to stop or ''pump and dump.'' Most medicines are compatible, and the way to confirm yours is to ask a doctor or pharmacist directly, rather than guess or stop feeding just in case. Whether or not you''re still breastfeeding, don''t stop or reschedule a prescribed medicine on your own — that''s a decision for your prescriber, not a guess to make at home. Combination over-the-counter products deserve a closer look than a single-ingredient one, since they bundle several ingredients together. For some medicines, timing a dose around feeds can help — but this isn''t universal, so it''s worth asking specifically about yours. This week is about building the habit of checking before starting something new, so it becomes routine rather than a source of ongoing worry.',
   'If you manage diabetes: some cold-and-flu products contain sugar or affect glucose — check the label or ask your pharmacist, and confirm any meal changes fit your care plan. If you''re prescribed a new regular medication for an ongoing condition: ask your prescriber specifically about breastfeeding compatibility at the time it''s prescribed, not after you''ve already started it. If your baby was born early or has a health condition of their own: mention this when asking about a new medicine, since it can occasionally change the advice. If you''re ever told to stop breastfeeding for a medicine and you''d rather not, it''s reasonable to ask whether a different medicine or formulation would let you continue — there''s often more than one option.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "If you''re currently breastfeeding: being prescribed or considering a new medicine doesn''t automatically mean you need to stop or ''pump and dump.'' Most medicines are compatible, and your doctor or pharmacist can confirm yours specifically, rather than you having to guess from a general rule. Whatever your feeding stage, don''t stop or reschedule a prescribed medicine on your own — any change to how or when you take it should come from your prescriber.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Who to actually ask, and the practical questions worth asking", "notes": "Your prescribing doctor, your pharmacist, or (if you''re breastfeeding) your baby''s paediatrician can each check a specific medicine against breastfeeding — mention that you''re breastfeeding whenever a new medicine is prescribed or recommended, rather than assuming they already know or that it won''t matter. It''s worth asking practical questions alongside the safety check: should this be taken with food or on an empty stomach? Does it need to be separated from supplements or particular foods? And if nausea from the medicine affects your appetite, what should you do about meals in the meantime? Worth knowing: A pharmacist is often the fastest person to ask about an over-the-counter product, and can check timing or an alternative formulation if there''s any concern.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of finely ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Multi-ingredient products need a closer look", "notes": "Combination products — like a multi-symptom cold-and-flu tablet — bundle several ingredients together, some of which may need more care than others while breastfeeding. Checking each ingredient, or asking your pharmacist to check the whole product for you, is more reliable than assuming a familiar brand name is automatically fine.", "breakfast_a": "Rava dosa (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Timing around feeds, if it''s suggested", "notes": "If you''re still breastfeeding: for some medicines, taking a dose right after a feed — rather than right before one — can reduce how much reaches your baby at the next feed. This isn''t necessary for every medicine, so ask specifically whether it applies to yours rather than adopting it as a blanket rule. If you''re not breastfeeding, food-and-medicine timing is still worth asking about, just for the reasons covered on Day 2 rather than feeding schedules. Worth knowing: If timing is recommended for your specific medicine, your prescriber or pharmacist can tell you what schedule actually fits both the medicine and your baby''s feeding pattern.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If you''re unwell while you sort this out", "notes": "Fluids and rest matter regardless of whether you''ve started a new medicine yet — a blocked nose, sore throat or general unwellness doesn''t need to wait on a medication decision before you take care of the basics. Smaller, more frequent meals can be easier to manage than a full plate while you''re feeling under the weather.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "A quick reference habit worth keeping", "notes": "Keeping your prescriber''s or pharmacist''s contact handy, and getting into the habit of asking ''is this fine while I''m breastfeeding?'' before starting anything new — prescription or over-the-counter — makes this a quick check rather than a source of ongoing worry each time it comes up.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Is there a medicine you''ve been putting off asking about — for yourself, not your child — that you could check on this week?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to treat checking a new medicine''s safety as a quick, ordinary question — rather than something that requires you to feel unwell enough to justify asking?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: your own health decisions deserve real information, not guesswork.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 77: Eating well while you rebuild your own routine, imperfectly (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   77,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well while you rebuild your own routine, imperfectly',
   '"An uneven week of meals while you find your footing again isn''t failure — it''s proof you''re rebuilding, and that''s exactly how rebuilding looks."',
   'After a stretch where your regular meals fell apart — illness, travel, a genuinely hard patch — getting back to a routine rarely happens all at once. Some days will look like your old rhythm; others won''t, and that''s a normal part of rebuilding rather than a sign you''re doing it wrong. This is a different kind of week from simply eating the same few meals out of habit — this is about permission for an uneven restart after disruption, not a steady routine you''re already in. This week focuses on one dependable anchor meal, a couple of easy backups for rough days, and noticing what''s already holding steady rather than only what isn''t.',
   'If you manage diabetes: follow your diabetes plan''s guidance for irregular meals, and contact your care team promptly if you can''t manage your intake or glucose within that plan — don''t wait to see if it settles. These A/B alternatives aren''t carbohydrate-matched, so check them against your own plan rather than assuming either option fits. If the disruption was an illness or recovery: give yourself the same patience you''d extend to anyone recovering — a fully steady routine can take longer to return than the illness itself did. If you''re still breastfeeding: your own energy and fluid needs continue through this uneven stretch — the anchor-meal approach below still applies just as much. If an unsteady routine has lasted more than a few weeks with no signs of settling, or comes with low mood or a loss of interest in things you''d usually enjoy, that''s worth mentioning to your doctor rather than waiting for it to resolve on its own.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can all sit comfortably in a regular week''s meals here. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. Repeating a combination you already enjoyed earlier in the week is completely fine — in fact it''s the point this week. If nothing here appeals or time is short, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "After a stretch where your regular meals fell apart — illness, travel, a genuinely hard patch — getting back to a routine rarely happens all at once. Some days will look like your old rhythm; others won''t, and that''s a normal part of rebuilding rather than a sign you''re doing it wrong.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Uneven progress is still progress", "notes": "A good day followed by a day where you barely manage two proper meals isn''t a setback — it''s what rebuilding an interrupted routine actually looks like for most people. Rather than ranking one day against another, it helps more to notice a single small, manageable improvement as you go.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a carrot sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a carrot sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of finely ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a lauki sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a lauki sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "One anchor meal, not a whole new system", "notes": "Rebuilding a routine goes more smoothly with one small, dependable anchor — a breakfast you always manage, say — rather than trying to restore every meal at once. This anchor meal is a tool for rebuilding the habit, not a stand-in for the rest of the day''s food — keep working toward regular meals across the whole day as you''re able, and once the anchor holds steady for a few days, the rest tends to follow more easily.", "breakfast_a": "Rava dosa (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "A missed meal doesn''t undo the ones before it", "notes": "Skipping a meal, or eating something far from what you''d planned, doesn''t erase whatever went right earlier in the day or week. The next meal is simply the next chance to eat well — not a make-up for the one you missed.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Keeping a couple of easy meals ready for the rough days", "notes": "Rebuilding goes easier with one or two meals you can put together almost without thinking, for the days when everything else has fallen through. This isn''t a lesser way to eat — it''s a practical support while the rest of your routine steadies.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Noticing what''s actually holding steady", "notes": "By now, some part of your routine has probably settled back in, even if the rest hasn''t — that''s worth noticing on its own terms, rather than only tracking what''s still uneven. Recognising what''s working helps you build outward from it, rather than starting over each day.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you. Looking back over this uneven week, what''s the one thing that actually held steady — and is that a fair place to build from next week?", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-tomato toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'What would it look like to notice one small, manageable improvement this week, rather than measuring yourself against any single day, good or bad?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: an imperfect routine, rebuilt patiently, still counts as real progress.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
