-- postpartum week 78: Eating well when your hair, skin or nails seem to be telling you something (CORRECTED — mother-centric rework)
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   78,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating well when your hair, skin or nails seem to be telling you something',
   '"Noticing a change is worth paying attention to — but one change, on its own, is a data point, not a diagnosis. A proper clinical assessment tells you far more than guessing ever will."',
   'Typical postpartum hair shedding (telogen effluvium) usually peaks a few months after birth and improves by the first birthday. At around eighteen months postpartum, hair loss that''s new, that hasn''t settled, or that''s actually increasing is different from that early pattern and worth an assessment rather than assuming it will resolve the same way on its own. Iron, protein, zinc and biotin are the nutrients most commonly discussed alongside hair, skin and nail changes — worth an honest look at your own plate, though none of them explains every case on its own. If a change feels persistent or more than you''d expect, a clinical assessment — with tests if your doctor thinks they''re indicated — is the way to actually find out what''s behind it. Not every cause is diagnosed by a blood test alone, and a symptom checklist or starting a supplement on your own isn''t a substitute for that assessment.',
   'If you already know you have low iron or a thyroid condition: follow your prescriber''s treatment plan for it — the meals here support that plan, they don''t replace it. If you''re on a restricted or specialised diet: ask your doctor or a dietitian whether it affects your iron, zinc, protein or biotin intake specifically, rather than assuming it does or doesn''t. If you''re still breastfeeding: breastfeeding on its own doesn''t automatically mean higher iron needs — what actually matters is your own iron stores, bleeding history and whether periods have returned. Include regular protein sources and adjust portions to your individual needs; this menu has not been calculated to confirm your daily protein requirement. Sudden patchy hair loss (rather than all-over thinning), or any hair, skin or nail change alongside fever, rash, joint pain, unusual fatigue or a heart rate that feels too fast or too slow, is different from ordinary postpartum shedding and is worth a prompt doctor visit rather than waiting it out.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs are useful sources of the iron, protein and zinc discussed this week. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding and eat fish, FDA/EPA guidance recommends two to three adult servings weekly from its "Best Choices" list — about 113 g per serving before cooking. Follow local advisories. This is an option for fish eaters, not a requirement to add fish.',
   'Choose either option, not both — these are alternatives, not two separate meals. The "if you''re still hungry" suggestions are suggestions, not limits — adjust everything to your own appetite. If nothing here appeals, reheated dal, rice and curd is an option if it suits your dietary needs and any care plan you follow. Portions below are adjustable starting points, not maximum allowances — scale to your own appetite, activity and any care plan you follow. A cup means a standard 240ml measuring cup. Unless a dish says otherwise: rice or another cooked grain eaten on its own is one level cup (about 150g); khichdi (grain and dal cooked together) is a heaped cup (about 250g total), of which dal makes up roughly a third; a dal, bean or lentil curry eaten as its own dish is three-quarters of a cup cooked (about 150g); a roti is one medium piece; an idli, dosa or chilla is 2-3 pieces, and toast is 2 slices. In a mixed rice or grain dish, the meat, fish, paneer or legume component is about 75-100g of that ingredient (roughly a palm-sized piece, or half a cup of cooked legumes), counted separately from the grain itself. Paneer, tofu, meat or fish eaten as a dish''s main item is 75-100g cooked (a palm-sized piece), or two eggs — these are different foods with different protein, iron and fat content, and are not nutritionally interchangeable just because they share a similar portion size. Curd or milk is a small bowl or glass (150-200ml); a cooked vegetable side is about a cup; nuts or seeds used as a topping are about a tablespoon (roughly 10-15g). The meals contribute different nutrients across the day; each individual meal or snack does not need to include every food group. Choose varied meals through the day and include a dependable protein source and a dependable calcium source at more than one meal — curd, milk or paneer — rather than relying on trace amounts in mixed dishes or an optional extra. If you avoid dairy, include measured portions of calcium-fortified alternatives or calcium-set tofu, checking the label. Adults generally need about 1,000 mg of calcium a day, including while breastfeeding — a single measured serving is a helpful building block, not the whole day''s requirement on its own. Food choices alone don''t rule out a need for supplementation — ask your own clinician what''s right for you.',
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Typical postpartum hair shedding (telogen effluvium) usually peaks a few months after birth and improves by the first birthday. At around eighteen months postpartum, hair loss that''s new, that hasn''t settled, or that''s actually increasing is different from that early pattern and is worth an assessment rather than assuming it will resolve the same way on its own.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "The nutrients most often discussed here", "notes": "Iron, protein, zinc and biotin are the nutrients most commonly discussed alongside hair, skin and nail changes — worth an honest look at your own plate, though none of them explains every case, and having enough of each doesn''t guarantee no shedding at all. Worth knowing: If you''re already taking a high-dose biotin or hair-growth supplement, mention it to whoever orders your blood tests — high-dose biotin can interfere with several common lab tests, including thyroid tests, and throw off the result.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla (2 pieces) with mint chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a rajma curry (3/4 cup) and a spinach sabzi", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a spinach sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted almonds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd (150g) with a spoon of finely ground flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor dal (3/4 cup) and a beetroot sabzi", "dinner_b": "Egg curry with roti (2 pieces) and a beetroot sabzi", "still_hungry": "A boiled egg"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "A clinical assessment, not a guess", "notes": "If a change feels persistent or more than you''d expect, a clinical assessment — which may include blood tests for iron stores, thyroid function or vitamin D among other things, if your doctor thinks they''re indicated — is the way to actually find out what, if anything, is behind it. Not every cause shows up on a blood test, so a doctor''s exam matters alongside it, not instead of it. Self-diagnosing from a symptom list and starting supplements on your own can mask what''s really going on and, in some cases, cause harm from excess intake.", "breakfast_a": "Rava dosa (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-capsicum toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a toor dal (3/4 cup) and a cauliflower sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken tikka (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A guava with roasted walnuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd (150g) with a few pomegranate seeds", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-spinach curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Chicken-and-methi curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Thyroid problems can show up this way too", "notes": "Thyroid problems are a common cause of hair, skin, energy and mood changes together — a reason a clinical assessment, rather than diet alone, is often the more useful next step when several signals show up at once, not only hair on its own. Postpartum thyroiditis specifically usually begins within the first year after birth, though its effects can persist longer, so a thyroid problem showing up now isn''t automatically that — it''s still worth asking your doctor to check. Worth knowing: if a thyroid or other condition is confirmed, follow your prescriber''s treatment plan — diet supports it but doesn''t replace it.", "breakfast_a": "Idli (3 pieces) with sambar and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a masoor-toor dal (3/4 cup) and a beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a beans sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with roasted chana (30g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a mandarin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a rajma-tomato curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-tomato curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk (200ml)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Skin and nail changes, the same way", "notes": "Dry skin or nails that chip or split more easily can have several ordinary causes — weather, frequent hand-washing, sleep loss — alongside any nutritional piece, so it''s worth not jumping to one explanation. A dermatologist or your regular doctor can help sort out which is which if it persists.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam (2 pieces) with coconut chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-onion bhurji (2 eggs) with toast (2 slices) and a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a chana-lauki curry (3/4 cup)", "lunch_b": "Mutton-and-spinach curry (100g mutton) with rice (1 cup cooked)", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with roasted sunflower seeds (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a few crushed mint leaves", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a masoor-methi dal (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with roti (2 pieces) and a beans sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Tracking a trend, not one snapshot", "notes": "A single day of noticing more shedding than usual, or one dry patch of skin, isn''t itself meaningful — what matters is whether it''s part of a genuine trend over weeks, and that''s better tracked with a photo or note every couple of weeks than by memory alone. Worth knowing: sudden patchy hair loss (rather than all-over thinning), or hair loss with fever, rash or joint pain, is different from ordinary postpartum shedding and is worth a prompt doctor visit rather than waiting it out.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla (2 pieces) with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-cucumber toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Khichdi (1 heaped cup) with curd", "lunch_b": "Fish curry (100g fish) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A mandarin with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of milk (200ml) with a banana", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana-carrot curry (3/4 cup)", "dinner_b": "Paneer-and-carrot curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces)", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A repeat-favourites day", "notes": "No forced choices today. Day 1''s combination is repeated below as a ready option — or swap in whichever meals from earlier in the week worked best for you.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha (1 cup) with a small bowl of curd (150g)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach toast (2 eggs, 2 slices) with a glass of milk (200ml)", "lunch_a": "Rice (1 cup cooked) with a moong dal (3/4 cup) and a cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (100g chicken) with rice (1 cup cooked) and a cabbage sabzi", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with roasted peanuts (15g) and a small bowl of curd (150g)", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of buttermilk (200ml, made with 100g curd) with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Roti (2 pieces) with a chana dal (3/4 cup) and a pumpkin sabzi", "dinner_b": "Paneer curry (75g paneer) with roti (2 pieces) and a pumpkin sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd (150g)"}]'::jsonb,
   'Is what you''re noticing a genuine weeks-long trend, or a single snapshot you''ve been carrying more worry about than the evidence yet calls for — and either way, is this worth bringing to your doctor at your next visit, or sooner if it''s on your mind?',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another piece of life beyond the first year — still grounded in the same idea: your own body''s signals are worth noticing and worth asking a professional about, not worth diagnosing alone.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;
